     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, for arbitrary input sequences
     6	`A:ValSeq` and `B:ValSeq`, loading the exact translated `common` definition
     7	and calling it with `list(A)` and `list(B)` reaches:
     8	
     9	- return value `ref(1)`;
    10	- heap location `0` containing
    11	  `list(commonAcc(A, B, .ValSeq))`; and
    12	- heap location `1` containing
    13	  `list(sortVS(commonAcc(A, B, .ValSeq)))`.
    14	
    15	`commonAcc` visits `A` in encounter order. It appends an element exactly when
    16	the fixed list-membership result says that the element occurs in `B` and does
    17	not yet occur in the accumulator. Thus it defines the duplicate-free
    18	intersection. The final ordering statement is conditional on the supplied
    19	`sortVS` contract: for supported mutually orderable values, `sortVS` is their
    20	ascending sort.
    21	
    22	This is a K reachability proof of partial correctness. It does not prove
    23	termination.
    24	
    25	# Formal claims
    26	
    27	`spec.k` contains three positive claims, all proved together:
    28	
    29	1. `member-fold` connects fixed execution of
    30	   `#memberAcc(V, list(S))` to `commonMember(V, S)`.
    31	2. `common-loop` is the loop circularity. From an unprocessed suffix `A` and
    32	   accumulator `ACC`, it preserves the exact continuation, bindings, stack,
    33	   exception state, and allocation state, changes only the loop target and the
    34	   result-list heap object, and leaves that object as
    35	   `commonAcc(A, B, ACC)`.
    36	3. `common-program` loads the exact function body, invokes it through normal
    37	   lookup/call/frame rules, executes the loop, calls the supplied `sorted`
    38	   builtin, and constrains the returned reference and both allocated lists.
    39	
    40	The entry claim has no `requires` clause: its execution characterization ranges
    41	over all `ValSeq` inputs in the supplied model. The human-facing sortedness
    42	interpretation is conditional on `sortVS`'s named contract.
    43	
    44	# Proof-extension inventory
    45	
    46	## `commonMember`
    47	
    48	- Class: definitional summary.
    49	- Semantic role: names the mathematical result of fixed list membership; it
    50	  does not rewrite a program term.
    51	- Domain: every `Val` and every finite `ValSeq`.
    52	- Equations: the empty and cons constructors are disjoint and exhaustive; the
    53	  recursive call is on the strict tail.
    54	- Matched context and state footprint: none; equations operate only on values.
    55	- Value influence: controls both intersection filtering and duplicate
    56	  suppression through `commonAcc`.
    57	- Value justification: the universal `member-fold` reachability claim executes
    58	  the supplied `#memberAcc/#iterNext/#memberCont` rules and produces exactly
    59	  `commonMember`.
    60	- Dependents: `member-fold`, `common-loop`, and `common-program`.
    61	- Validation: the positive proof is `#Top`; the false-summary mutation and
    62	  concrete/differential tests distinguish true from false membership outcomes.
    63	
    64	## Guarded Boolean simplification rule
    65	
    66	- Exact rule: when `notBool (E ==K V)` holds,
    67	  `(E ==K V) orBool B` simplifies to `B`.
    68	- Class: derived lemma.
    69	- Semantic role: solver normalization only; it replaces no execution.
    70	- Domain: arbitrary `E:Val`, `V:Val`, and `B:Bool` under the displayed guard.
    71	- Context and state footprint: no configuration context and no state cells.
    72	- Justification: under the guard, the left disjunct is false, so the equation
    73	  is the Boolean identity `false or B = B`. Its overlap with the built-in
    74	  concrete Boolean simplification has the same right-hand side.
    75	- Dependents: `member-fold` and therefore the two larger claims.
    76	- Validation: the bridge-free `member-fold` claim closes universally.
    77	
    78	## `commonAcc`
    79	
    80	- Class: definitional summary.
    81	- Semantic role: names the mathematical result of the remaining loop; it does
    82	  not intercept `For`, `#loop`, `Call`, or heap mutation.
    83	- Domain: every triple of finite `ValSeq` values.
    84	- Equations: empty and cons cases are disjoint and exhaustive. The cons case
    85	  makes an explicit total Boolean choice and recurses on the strict tail.
    86	- Context and state footprint: none in its equations.
    87	- Value influence: fixes heap location `0`, the argument to `sortVS`, heap
    88	  location `1`, and consequently the returned list's contents.
    89	- Value justification: `common-loop` executes the exact loop body with fixed
    90	  name lookup, short-circuiting membership checks, method binding, `append`
    91	  heap mutation, and loop control, reaching exactly `commonAcc`.
    92	- Dependents: `common-loop` and `common-program`.
    93	- Validation: `spec-value-mutation.k` assigns the opposite empty result to the
    94	  realizable input `[1], [1]`; `kprove` rejects it and exposes the actual
    95	  `[1]` heaps.
    96	
    97	## `member-fold`
    98	
    99	- Class: derived auxiliary reachability theorem/circularity.
   100	- Semantic role: summarizes fixed membership execution for reuse; it is a
   101	  `claim`, not an operational `rule`.
   102	- Complete match: `#memberAcc(V, list(S))` at the head of an arbitrary
   103	  continuation, with all other configuration cells preserved and arbitrary.
   104	- Justification scope and containment: the claim itself is universal over that
   105	  exact continuation frame and all other cells. Membership reads no cells and
   106	  has no abrupt control effect.
   107	- State footprint: none.
   108	- Value influence: produces the Boolean used by the source condition and
   109	  `commonAcc`.
   110	- Justification: induction on `S` by the supplied iterator and membership
   111	  rules plus the truthful `commonMember` equations.
   112	- Dependents: `common-loop` and `common-program`.
   113	- Validation: machine-checked as part of the positive `#Top` run.
   114	
   115	## `common-loop`
   116	
   117	- Class: derived loop-invariant reachability theorem/circularity.
   118	- Semantic role: executes and inductively summarizes the source loop; it is not
   119	  an operational rule.
   120	- Complete match: environment `1`; local bindings for `l1`, `l2`, `result`,
   121	  and `item`; result object at heap location `0`; heap location counter `1`;
   122	  exact caller frame; exact
   123	  `Return(Call(Name("sorted"), Name("result"))) .Stmts ~> #endcall`
   124	  continuation; and unchanged return, exception, and exit-code cells.
   125	- Justification scope and containment: its match and theorem scope are the same.
   126	  The module-scope map is universally framed and preserved because the loop
   127	  accesses only the pinned local bindings.
   128	- State footprint: reads `l2` and the result list; writes the local `item`
   129	  binding and heap location `0`; preserves `l1`, `l2`, globals, allocation
   130	  counters, stack, return state, exception state, and exit code.
   131	- Value influence: fixes the unsorted accumulator consumed by `sorted`.
   132	- Justification: base case uses the empty iterator; the step executes target
   133	  binding, both fixed membership folds, short-circuit control, optional
   134	  `append`, and `#loopLbl`, then reapplies the circularity to the strict suffix.
   135	- Dependents: `common-program`.
   136	- Control validation: `spec-body-mutation.k` removes duplicate suppression.
   137	  On `[1, 1], [1]`, fixed execution reaches `[1, 1]` and rejects the original
   138	  theorem's `[1]` result.
   139	
   140	## Syntax macros
   141	
   142	`commonLoopBody()` and `commonBody()` are compile-time abbreviations for the
   143	exact constructors in `solution.mpy`. They add no equation about values and no
   144	runtime rewrite. Regenerating `solution.mpy` from `solution.py` and the
   145	body-sensitivity mutation check provide the identity evidence.
   146	
   147	## Supplied `sortVS`
   148	
   149	- Class: trusted primitive supplied by `reference-semantics/semantics/sort.k`;
   150	  it is not a proof-local extension.
   151	- Semantic role: the fixed `sorted` builtin allocates a new list containing
   152	  `sortVS(VS)`. Symbolic proofs keep it opaque; the LLVM leg uses insertion
   153	  sort for integer and string lists.
   154	- Domain used for the human-facing conclusion: homogeneous, mutually
   155	  orderable integer or modeled string lists.
   156	- State/control footprint: reads the result list, allocates a fresh sorted list,
   157	  increments `heapLoc`, and returns its reference. These surrounding effects
   158	  execute in fixed semantics.
   159	- Value influence: fixes the order of every final result element.
   160	- Named contract: `sortVS(VS)` is the ascending permutation of `VS` on its
   161	  supported domain.
   162	- Dependents: `common-program` and the human-facing “sorted” conclusion.
   163	- Evidence: eight LLVM assertions cover prompt examples, duplicates, empty
   164	  lists, negative integers, and strings; the independent CPython differential
   165	  suite covers 3,200 ordered input pairs with zero mismatches.
   166	- Limitation: no universal K theorem of the sorting algorithm is claimed.
   167	
