denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/318/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/321/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/323/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/338/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/342/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/344/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/347/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/348/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/351/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/445/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/446/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/task/447/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/map_files’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/268/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/404/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/405/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/406/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/407/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/408/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/409/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/410/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/411/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/412/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/413/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/414/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/415/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/416/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/417/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/418/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/419/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/420/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/421/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/422/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/task/526/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/map_files’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/404/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/527/task/527/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/527/map_files’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/527/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/530/task/530/fdinfo’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/530/map_files’: Permission denied\nfind: ‘../proc/530/fdinfo’: Permission denied\n---\nname: writing-spec\ndescription: 'Use when you have code and its K semantics and need to state what to prove in spec.k. Triggered by \"write a spec for this program\", \"what should the preconditions be\", \"turn my postcondition into a K claim\", or \"draft spec.k\".'\n---\n\n## Before writing: check `semantics.k`\n\n`spec.k` imports and compiles against `semantics.k`. Confirm its status first:\n\n| State | Action |\n|---|---|\n| `semantics.k` exists and compiles | Proceed with this skill |\n| `semantics.k` exists but not yet smoke-tested | Proceed; note that spec errors may trace back to semantics bugs |\n| `semantics.k` does not exist | Use `writing-semantics` first, then return here |\n\nAlso confirm that concrete program identifiers used by the claims parse as\nidentifiers rather than K rule variables. See\n[K syntax and operational semantics](../shared/k-syntax.md#grammar).\n\n---\n\n## What a spec is\n\nA K spec states **partial correctness**: *if* the program terminates and the precondition holds, then the postcondition holds at termination. It says nothing about whether the program terminates; that is a separate liveness question.\n\nKeep three activities distinct (see `using-kit`): **Verification** closes\nreachability claims under the supplied theory; **Soundness audit** checks proof\nextensions; and **Validation** checks theorem scope and evidence against the\nintended property.\n\n---\n\n## Record the validation scope\n\nBefore drafting claims, record four items that validating-proof will check:\n\n| Item | Record |\n|---|---|\n| Program boundary | Exact entry computation and program-defined operations included in the theorem |\n| Input domain | Types, guards, well-formedness conditions, and excluded inputs |\n| Observable final state | Result and every state cell the intended property observes |\n| Intended property | Plain-language result expected after termination |\n\nThe spec may frame cells that are intentionally irrelevant, but the scope record\nmust say why they are unobserved. Do not silently omit a cell whose change is\npart of the intended behavior.\n\n---\n\n## Pre- and postconditions\n\nConditions are constraints on cell contents, written as `requires` and `ensures` clauses on a `claim`:\n\n- **`requires`** — the precondition: a Boolean expression over the LHS cell values. The claim is only attempted when this holds.\n- **`ensures`** — the postcondition: a Boolean expression over the RHS cell values. May introduce existential variables written `?X` (e.g. `ensures ?R ==Int sumTo(N0)`). Any RHS variable not bound on the LHS must use `?`; omitting it is a hard K error.\n\nPreconditions and postconditions are constraints on the full K configuration (the `<k>` cell and every state cell) before and after the rewrite — not just function inputs/outputs in the ordinary sense.\n\n**Programs that produce a return value** — use `ensures` to constrain it. Example shape for a claim where the computation leaves a result `?R` in `<k>`:\n\n```k\nclaim [my-prog]:\n      <k> myProgram(N0:Int) => ?R:Int </k>\n      <state> S </state>\n  requires N0 >=Int 0\n  ensures  ?R ==Int sumTo(N0)\n```\n\n**Programs that terminate with a state** (no return value) — express the postcondition as the RHS of the state cells directly, without `ensures`:\n\n```k\n<state> .Map => n |-> 0 s |-> sumTo(N0) </state>\n```\n\n---\n\n## The two claims in `spec.k`\n\nA complete spec contains one **entry claim** for the whole program plus one **loop-invariant claim per loop**. The single-loop program used throughout this kit therefore has exactly two claims; a program with two loops needs three, and a nested loop needs its own invariant claim, which the outer loop's proof then uses to step over the inner loop.\n\n### 1. Entry (whole-program) claim\n\nStates what the full program does from its initial configuration to termination.\n\n```k\nclaim [sum-prog]:\n      <k> n = N0:Int ; s = 0 ;\n          while (n > 0) { s = s + n ; n = n - 1 ; } => .K ...</k>\n      <state> .Map => n |-> 0 s |-> sumTo(N0) </state>\n  requires N0 >=Int 0\n```\n\n- LHS: the full program text in `<k>`; initial state (`.Map` = empty).\n- RHS: `.K` in `<k>` means all computation is done; the final state maps are the postcondition.\n- `...` in `<k>` is a frame variable for the rest of the continuation.\n- The state uses a closed map (no `...`) when exactly those keys must be present.\n\n### 2. Loop-invariant (circularity) claim\n\nStates what the loop does at the loop head, given symbolic accumulator values. This is the coinductive hypothesis — kprove uses the claim to discharge the loop by applying it to itself.\n\n```k\nclaim [loop-inv]:\n      <k> while (n > 0) { s = s + n ; n = n - 1 ; } => .K ...</k>\n      <state> n |-> (N:Int => 0)\n              s |-> (S:Int => S +Int sumTo(N)) </state>\n  requires N >=Int 0\n```\n\n- Starting at the loop head with `n |-> N` and `s |-> S` (both symbolic), `N >= 0`.\n- The loop terminates with `n |-> 0` and `s |-> S + sumTo(N)`.\n- The `=>` inside each cell maps old to new: `N:Int => 0` means `n` starts at `N` and ends at `0`.\n\nBoth claims live in a single spec module that only contains `claim`s (and optionally `[simplification]` rules). Plain `rule` statements in a spec module are a K compiler error.\n\n```k\nrequires \"verification.k\"\n\nmodule SPEC\n  imports VERIFICATION\n\n  claim [loop-inv]: ...\n  claim [sum-prog]: ...\nendmodule\n```\n\n---\n\n## The loop invariant\n\nThe loop invariant ties the running accumulator (`s`) and the remaining count (`n`) to the postcondition through a summary function (e.g. `sumTo`), parametrized by the mid-loop state so it covers every iteration, not just the initial call.\n\nFor the step-by-step derivation method and how to choose between a\nsolver-friendly closed form and a necessary recursive definition, see\n[`k-proof-technique`](../k-proof-technique/SKILL.md).\n\n---\n\n## Adequacy hand-off\n\nOnce the spec is drafted, `proving-spec` owns verification, while\n`validating-proof` owns the independent soundness audit and validation. Under\nthe shared\n[proof-extension soundness contract](../shared/proof-extension-soundness.md),\n`validating-proof` applies the Gate A/B/C audit. Do not duplicate those checks\nhere.\n\nMachine-check the spec structure with a focused `kprove` attempt even before\nthe proof is expected to close. Early parser and module errors are cheaper to\nfix before adding lemmas.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n- [K functions, claims, and proof modules](../shared/k-claims.md) — claim\n  syntax, `requires`/`ensures`, labels, and proof-module restrictions.\n- `proving-spec` — next step: add `verification.k` (summary function + kompiled definition) and make the claims pass `kprove`.\n- `validating-proof` — independently audits proof-extension soundness and\n  validates intent, trust, and evidence for a passing proof.\n---\nname: proving-spec\ndescription: 'Use when a K spec does not pass kprove yet — the proof hangs, diverges, prints WarnStuckClaimState, or exits non-zero — and you need to make it pass.'\n---\n\n## What this skill does\n\n`spec.k` is a set of K reachability claims. `kprove` attempts to prove each claim by symbolically executing from the LHS and checking it reaches the RHS under the given conditions. When the proof fails, `kprove` exits non-zero and prints a residual configuration — the state it reached but could not close. This skill provides the tools to read that residual and extend `verification.k` until the proof closes.\n\nUse `writing-spec` before this skill if `spec.k` is not yet written.\n\n---\n\n## Why proofs get stuck\n\n`kprove` closes a claim by rewriting the LHS to the RHS. It gets stuck when:\n\n- **Symbolic arithmetic does not reduce.** The backend cannot discharge the\n  remaining obligation as written. Express the result through a summary whose\n  defining equations or lemmas fit a theory the prover can use.\n- **The loop-invariant circularity does not fire.** The recurring symbolic\n  configuration does not match the invariant claim's left-hand side closely\n  enough for the claim to apply, so the prover expands another iteration. See\n  [circularity debugging](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/circularity-not-applying.md).\n- **A helper rule is in the spec module instead of `verification.k`.** Plain `rule` statements in the spec module are a K compiler error; only `claim`s and `[simplification]` rules are allowed there.\n\nThe repair loop below addresses each of these systematically.\n\n---\n\n## Before adding a proof extension\n\nRead the [proof-extension soundness contract](../shared/proof-extension-soundness.md).\nFor the proposed change, answer these questions from the residual:\n\n1. What fixed-semantics step or mathematical obligation is missing?\n2. Which extension class applies?\n3. **Does this replace execution?** If yes, identify the exact binding, body,\n   evaluation steps, control behavior, and state footprint being replaced.\n4. What is the complete matched context, including the active continuation,\n   control stack, bindings, guards, and framed cells?\n5. Is every frame, wildcard, or omitted cell equally general in the theorem or\n   assumption that supplies the justification domain?\n6. Does the extension introduce abrupt control such as return, frame popping,\n   an exception, or loop control? If so, what proves the same continuation is\n   discarded, preserved, or unwound under fixed semantics?\n7. Does the extension's value affect a branch, result, observable state,\n   exception, or postcondition? If so, what machine-checked connection theorem\n   proves that fixed semantics produces that value over the complete domain?\n8. Does the same fresh or opaque symbol occur in both an operational bridge and\n   a summary or postcondition? That dependency is circular unless independently\n   connected to fixed execution.\n9. What guard makes every new equation true, and do its cases overlap existing\n   equations consistently?\n10. Which claim depends on the extension, and what theorem or named trust\n   assumption justifies it?\n\nComplete the contract's extension record before editing `verification.k`. If a\nprogram-defined operation cannot be connected to execution, strengthen the\ninvariant, add an auxiliary execution claim, route a genuine language-model gap\nto `writing-semantics`, or continue the same-session repair loop below.\n\nDo not introduce a result-bearing oracle to make a residual disappear. Exact\nsyntax and bindings prove where a bridge applies, not which value it returns.\nIf no machine-checked connection theorem or truthful definition fixes that\nvalue, let fixed semantics execute, strengthen the invariant, or narrow the\ntheorem honestly.\n\nFor an operational bridge, place its complete LHS and RHS beside the exact\nsupporting theorem and check that the bridge match domain is contained in the\njustification domain. A theorem over one trailing computation does not justify\nframing an arbitrary `<k>` continuation. Narrow the bridge to the proved context\nor prove a separate context theorem; comments, priority, and a correct summary\nvalue do not repair scope widening.\n\nBefore admitting any operational bridge, prove a bridge-free universal\nconnection theorem over its complete match domain. That theorem must not import\nthe proposed bridge; prove it with fixed semantics and independently justified\ntheory. Finite tests do not satisfy this precondition.\n\n## Same-session Gate A repair loop\n\nWhen `validating-proof` reports a Gate A failure, continue within the current\nagent invocation and preserve the current solution and proof artifacts:\n\n1. **First, remove or disable the offending extension.** Do not treat `#Top`\n   obtained through that extension as a usable proof state.\n2. Recompile and rerun without it, then inspect the genuine residual produced\n   by fixed semantics and the remaining justified theory.\n3. **Prefer fixed-semantics execution** and address the residual without an\n   execution-bypassing rewrite.\n4. **Prove an exact auxiliary execution theorem** before any operational bridge,\n   covering every configuration in the proposed bridge's match domain without\n   importing that bridge.\n5. Strengthen the invariant or use a truthful definitional summary when that\n   expresses the source computation exactly.\n6. If the original intent is broader than the sound theorem available, narrow\n   the theorem honestly and allow Gate B to report `SOUND-BUT-LIMITED`.\n7. Produce terminal `Incomplete work` only when repair attempts expose an\n   evidenced hard blocker under the shared contract.\n\nAfter a repaired construction reaches `#Top`, return to `validating-proof` and\nrestart Gate A. This loop is construction inside the original one-shot\ninvocation, not a retry or later audit-feedback attempt.\n\n---\n\n## Summary functions\n\nA **summary function** names the mathematical result computed by the loop.\nDefine it in `verification.k` as a `[function, total]` declaration with\nequations that cover all inputs. The example below has a convenient closed\nform; other results may require a recursive definition.\n\n```k\n// verification.k\nrequires \"semantics.k\"\n\nmodule VERIFICATION-SYNTAX\n  imports INT\n  syntax Int ::= sumTo(Int) [function, total]\nendmodule\n\nmodule VERIFICATION\n  imports VERIFICATION-SYNTAX\n  imports SEMANTICS\n  imports INT\n  imports BOOL\n\n  rule sumTo(N) => (N +Int 1) *Int N /Int 2  requires N >=Int 0\n  rule sumTo(N) => 0                          requires N  <Int 0\nendmodule\n```\n\n- `[function]` — this is a function symbol, not a rewrite rule.\n- `[total]` — the equations cover all inputs; the prover may assume totality.\n- Two guarded equations split on the sign so coverage is complete.\n\n**Choose the simplest faithful form the prover can use.** Prefer an equivalent\nclosed form when it lies in a solver-supported theory. If no faithful closed\nform exists, keep the recursive definition; do not replace it with an\ninaccurate formula merely to make the proof close. Use explicit base/step\nequations plus targeted induction, folding, or summary lemmas. A recursive\nevaluator can still unfold indefinitely on a symbolic argument, so diagnose\nthat mechanism rather than rejecting recursion itself. See\n[`k-proof-technique`](../k-proof-technique/SKILL.md) and\n[symbolic-recursion debugging](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/symbolic-recursion.md).\n\n**Add `[simplification]` lemmas** for algebraic facts the prover cannot derive on its own. A `[simplification]` rule fires during symbolic simplification, not during normal rewriting. Keep them in `verification.k` (or, if they apply only to a specific proof, in the spec module):\n\n```k\nrule (X +Int Y) ==Int X +Int Z => Y ==Int Z  [simplification]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Invariants and lemmas\n\nThe loop-invariant claim in `spec.k` is the loop invariant. When the proof is stuck on the invariant's inductive step, you have two options:\n\n1. **Strengthen the invariant.** Add a conjunct to the `requires` or express a tighter postcondition.\n2. **Add a lemma.** Add a `[simplification]` rule to `verification.k` that lets the prover discharge the residual algebraic obligation.\n\nKeep the invariant and the summary function consistent: the invariant postcondition (`s |-> S +Int sumTo(N)`) and the summary function definition (`sumTo(N) = N*(N+1)/2`) must describe the same quantity.\n\n---\n\n## The repair loop\n\n```\nrun kprove → read the residual → add one lemma or strengthen invariant → re-run\n```\n\n**Step 1: run `kprove`.**\n\n```bash\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.nix-profile/bin:$PATH\"\nkprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC\n```\n\nA bare `#Top` on stdout with exit code 0 means the claim closed under the\nsupplied theory; it is necessary but insufficient for a validated proof.\nFailure is a non-zero exit with a `WarnStuckClaimState` warning and a residual\nconfiguration on stdout.\n\n**Step 2: read the residual.** The residual is the symbolic configuration `kprove` reached but could not match against the claim's RHS. Compare its term shape to the RHS. The mismatch locates what is missing.\n\n**Step 3: bound and compare the trace.** Isolate one claim, choose a depth that\nreturns promptly, and compare residual configurations at nearby increasing\nbounds. Use the symptom router and bounded-inspection procedure in\n[the troubleshooting index](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/index.md).\n\n**Step 4: add one classified change.** Complete the extension checkpoint, then\nadd the narrowest guarded lemma, summary equation, auxiliary claim, or invariant\nstrengthening that addresses the residual. Recompile and rerun the focused claim.\n\nRecord the extension even when the proof reaches `#Top`; prover success does not\nvalidate the added theory.\n\nRecompile `verification.k`:\n\n```bash\nkompile --backend haskell verification.k \\\n        --main-module VERIFICATION \\\n        --syntax-module SEMANTICS-SYNTAX \\\n        --output-definition verification-kompiled\n```\n\n**Important hygiene:**\n- Prove claims in order: the loop invariant first (`--claims SPEC.loop-inv`), then the whole-program claim. A passing loop invariant makes the whole-program proof fast.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n- [Proof-extension soundness contract](../shared/proof-extension-soundness.md) —\n  construction-time classification and extension-record requirements.\n- [kprove debugging index](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/index.md) — symptom-based\n  routing for residuals and command failures.\n- [K functions, claims, and proof modules](../shared/k-claims.md) —\n  `[function]`/`[simplification]`, claim syntax, and labels.\n- [Running the K tools](../shared/running-k.md) — build commands and result\n  interpretation.\n- `writing-spec` — precedes this skill; produces `spec.k`.\n- `validating-proof` — follows this skill; it independently rebuilds the\n  extension inventory from the proof files rather than trusting the construction\n  record, then applies Gate A — real-program soundness, Gate B — intent adequacy,\n  and Gate C — trust and evidence auditability.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
30:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:34:07.614Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_088a97266d078c35016a644acc43e08196b9ab2a1caccd953f","status":"completed","call_id":"call_t1JZl5UNEwFElgCxCnjeYu7j","name":"exec","input":"const files = [\n\"py2mpy.py\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/syntax.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/core.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/functions.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/call.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/list.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/iter.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/builtins.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/sort.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/set.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/operators.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k\",\n\"reference-semantics/semantics/concrete.k\"\n];\nconst out = await Promise.all(files.map(async f => {\n  const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:`wc -l ${f}; sed -n '1,1000p' ${f}`,workdir:\"/workspace\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:30000});\n  return `===== ${f} =====\\n${r.output}`;\n}));\ntext(out.join(\"\\n\"));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
31:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:34:07.829Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_t1JZl5UNEwFElgCxCnjeYu7j","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 21335)\nTotal output lines: 1756\n\n===== py2mpy.py =====\n434 py2mpy.py\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"py2mpy — a *pure* CPython-AST -> K-constructor transliterator.\n\nReads a .py file and prints the matching `.mpy` term: a Lisp-indented tree of K\nconstructors that a (future) K semantics will parse. The design rule is strict:\n\n    PURE TRANSLITERATION. Every Python AST node maps to exactly one K\n    constructor with the same structure. The front-end makes NO semantic\n    decisions — it does not desugar, simplify, normalize, or \"interpret\"\n    anything. All meaning lives in the K semantics that consumes the term.\n\nConsequences of that rule (vs. a \"helpful\" front-end):\n\n  * `is` / `is not` transliterate to `CmpOp(\"is\", ...)` / `CmpOp(\"is not\", ...)`\n    — NOT to `==` / `!=`. K decides what identity means.\n  * a chained comparison `a < b < c` stays a single `Compare` carrying the\n    chain (`Compare(a, CmpOp(\"<\", b), CmpOp(\"<\", c))`) — it is NOT pre-expanded\n    into `a < b and b < c`.\n  * a method call `x.append(v)` stays `Call(Attribute(Name(\"x\"), \"append\"), v)`\n    — there is no special `MethodCall` constructor folding the receiver in.\n  * `a[i] = v` stays `Assign(Subscript(Name(\"a\"), Name(\"i\")), v)` — the target\n    is just an expression in store position; no special `SubscriptAssign`.\n\nThe only choices the transliterator makes are unavoidably *syntactic* — e.g. a\n`bool` literal becomes `Bool(...)` not `Int(...)`, because that is the literal's\nactual Python type as read off the AST, not a semantic judgement.\n\nAnything outside the covered subset raises `Unsupported`, naming the node, so\ncoverage gaps fail loudly instead of being silently dropped.\n\nUsage:\n    python scripts/py2mpy.py FILE.py          # print the .mpy term (default)\n    python scripts/py2mpy.py FILE.py --ast    # print the Python AST (ast.dump)\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport symtable\nimport ast\nimport json\nimport sys\n\n\nclass Unsupported(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised for any AST node the transliterator does not cover.\"\"\"\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Term model: a .mpy term is a tree of these three node kinds.\n#   Ctor(name, *args)  -> `name(arg, arg, ...)`  (comma-separated args)\n#   Seq([item, ...])   -> a statement list: juxtaposed, no parens, no commas\n#   str                -> a leaf token already in K surface form\n#                         (a quoted string `\"op\"`, a number `0`, `true`, `Break`)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nclass Ctor:\n    __slots__ = (\"name\", \"args\")\n\n    def __init__(self, name: str, *args):\n        self.name = name\n        self.args = list(args)\n\n\nclass Seq:\n    __slots__ = (\"items\",)\n\n    def __init__(self, items):\n        self.items = list(items)\n\n\ndef q(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"A Python string as a K String token (double-quoted, escaped).\"\"\"\n    return json.dumps(s)\n\n\n# --- Operator spellings (string tokens carried inside constructors) ----------\n# These are transliterations of the operator *symbol*, not its meaning.\n_BINOP = {\n    ast.Add: \"+\", ast.Sub: \"-\", ast.Mult: \"*\", ast.Div: \"/\",\n    ast.FloorDiv: \"//\", ast.Mod: \"%\", ast.Pow: \"**\",\n    ast.BitOr: \"|\", ast.BitAnd: \"&\", ast.BitXor: \"^\",\n    ast.LShift: \"<<\", ast.RShift: \">>\", ast.MatMult: \"@\",\n}\n_UNARY = {ast.USub: \"-\", ast.UAdd: \"+\", ast.Not: \"not\", ast.Invert: \"~\"}\n_BOOLOP = {ast.And: \"and\", ast.Or: \"or\"}\n_CMPOP = {\n    ast.Eq: \"==\", ast.NotEq: \"!=\", ast.Lt: \"<\", ast.LtE: \"<=\",\n    ast.Gt: \">\", ast.GtE: \">=\", ast.Is: \"is\", ast.IsNot: \"is not\",\n    ast.In: \"in\", ast.NotIn: \"not in\",\n}\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Closure scopes (Python's own symtable, faithfully exposed)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# (name, lineno) -> (cellvars, freevars, nested). A def/lambda gets the\n# ANNOTATED node form iff it is nested inside a function or has cells/frees;\n# module-level capture-free defs keep the old shape (zero churn).\nSCOPES: dict = {}\n\n\ndef _frees(table):\n    return set(table.get_frees()) if isinstance(table, symtable.Function) else set()\n\n\ndef _deep_frees(table):\n    out = _frees(table)\n    for g in table.get_children():\n        out |= _deep_frees(g) - set(table.get_locals())\n    return out\n\n\ndef _walk_symtable(table, depth=0):\n    for child in table.get_children():\n        if child.get_type() in (\"function\", \"lambda\"):\n            frees = sorted(_frees(child))\n            cells = sorted(\n                s for s in child.get_locals()\n                if any(s in _deep_frees(g) for g in child.get_children())\n            )\n            SCOPES[(child.get_name(), child.get_lineno())] = (\n                cells, frees, depth > 0 or table.get_type() != \"module\")\n        _walk_symtable(child, depth + (1 if child.get_type() in (\"function\", \"lambda\") else 0))\n\n\ndef scope_info(name, lineno):\n    return SCOPES.get((name, lineno))\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Expressions\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef emit_expr(node: ast.expr):\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Constant):\n        v = node.value\n        if v is None:\n            return \"NoneVal\"\n        if isinstance(v, bool):                 # MUST precede int (bool ⊂ int)\n            return Ctor(\"Bool\", \"true\" if v else \"false\")\n        if isinstance(v, int):\n            return Ctor(\"Int\", str(v))\n        if isinstance(v, float):\n            return Ctor(\"Float\", repr(v))\n        if isinstance(v, str):\n            return Ctor(\"Str\", q(v))\n        raise Unsupported(f\"Constant {type(v).__name__}\")\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Name):\n        return Ctor(\"Name\", q(node.id))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Lambda):\n        a = node.args\n        if a.vararg or a.kwarg or a.kwonlyargs or a.defaults or a.kw_defaults:\n            raise Unsupported(\"complex lambda signature\")\n        names = [p.arg for p in (a.posonlyargs + a.args)]\n        params = Ctor(\"Params\", *(q(n) for n in names))\n        info = scope_info(\"lambda\", node.lineno)\n        if info and (info[0] or info[1] or info[2]):\n            cells, frees, _ = info\n            return Ctor(\"Lambda\", params,\n                        Ctor(\"CellVars\", *(q(n) for n in cells)),\n                        Ctor(\"FreeVars\", *(q(n) for n in frees)),\n                        emit_expr(node.body))\n        return Ctor(\"Lambda\", params, emit_expr(node.body))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.BinOp):\n        op = _BINOP.get(type(node.op))\n        if op is None:\n            raise Unsupported(f\"BinOp {type(node.op).__name__}\")\n        return Ctor(\"BinOp\", q(op), emit_expr(node.left), emit_expr(node.right))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.UnaryOp):\n        op = _UNARY.get(type(node.op))\n        if op is None:\n            raise Unsupported(f\"UnaryOp {type(node.op).__name__}\")\n        return Ctor(\"UnaryOp\", q(op), emit_expr(node.operand))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.BoolOp):\n        op = _BOOLOP[type(node.op)]\n        return Ctor(\"BoolOp\", q(op), *(emit_expr(v) for v in node.values))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Compare):\n        # Faithful, non-desugared: left operand + a chain of (op, comparator).\n        # Single comparison => one CmpOp; `a < b < c` => two CmpOps.\n        chain = [\n            Ctor(\"CmpOp\", q(_op_or_raise(_CMPOP, op, \"Compare\")), emit_expr(c))\n            for op, c in zip(node.ops, node.comparators)\n        ]\n        return Ctor(\"Compare\", emit_expr(node.left), *chain)\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.List):\n        return Ctor(\"ListExpr\", *(emit_expr(e) for e in node.elts))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Tuple):\n        return Ctor(\"TupleExpr\", *(emit_expr(e) for e in node.elts))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Set):\n        return Ctor(\"SetExpr\", *(emit_expr(e) for e in node.elts))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Dict):\n        entries = []\n        for k, v in zip(node.keys, node.values):\n            if k is None:\n                raise Unsupported(\"dict unpacking {**x}\")\n            entries.append(Ctor(\"Entry\", emit_expr(k), emit_expr(v)))\n        return Ctor(\"DictExpr\", *entries)\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Subscript):\n        return Ctor(\"Subscript\", emit_expr(node.value), emit_index(node.slice))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Attribute):\n        return Ctor(\"Attribute\", emit_expr(node.value), q(node.attr))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Call):\n        if any(isinstance(a, ast.Starred) for a in node.args):\n            raise Unsupported(\"*args unpacking\")\n        if any(kw.arg is None for kw in node.keywords):\n            raise Unsupported(\"**kwargs unpacking\")\n        # Call grammar is `Call(Expr, Exprs)` — the comma is mandatory, so a zero-arg\n        # call must render as `Call(f, )` (empty Exprs), NOT `Call(f)` (which won't parse).\n        args = [emit_expr(a) for a in node.args]\n        args += [Ctor(\"KwArg\", q(kw.arg), emit_expr(kw.value)) for kw in node.keywords]\n        args = args or [\"\"]\n        return Ctor(\"Call\", emit_expr(node.func), *args)\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.IfExp):\n        # AST field order: test, body, orelse — kept verbatim.\n        return Ctor(\"IfExp\", emit_expr(node.test),\n                    emit_expr(node.body), emit_expr(node.orelse))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.ListComp):\n        return Ctor(\"ListComp\", emit_expr(node.elt), _gens(node.generators))\n    if isinstance(node, ast.SetComp):\n        return Ctor(\"SetComp\", emit_expr(node.elt), _gens(node.generators))\n    if isinstance(node, ast.GeneratorExp):\n        return Ctor(\"GenExp\", emit_expr(node.elt), _gens(node.generators))\n    if isinstance(node, ast.DictComp):\n        return Ctor(\"DictComp\", emit_expr(node.key), emit_expr(node.value),\n                    _gens(node.generators))\n\n    raise Unsupported(f\"Expr {type(node).__name__}\")\n\n\ndef _op_or_raise(table, op, label):\n    spelled = table.get(type(op))\n    if spelled is None:\n        raise Unsupported(f\"{label} {type(op).__name__}\")\n    return spelled\n\n\ndef _compfor(g):\n    \"\"\"A single generator -> CompFor(target, iter, <ifs as Exprs; .Exprs if none>).\"\"\"\n    if getattr(g, \"is_async\", 0):\n        raise Unsupported(\"async comprehension\")\n    ifs = [emit_expr(c) for c in g.ifs] or [Ctor(\"Bool\", \"true\")]\n    return Ctor(\"CompFor\", emit_expr(g.target), emit_expr(g.iter), *ifs)\n\n\ndef _gens(generators):\n    \"\"\"The generator list -> a juxtaposed CompFors (a Seq of CompFor).\"\"\"\n    return Seq([_compfor(g) for g in generators])\n\n\ndef emit_index(node: ast.expr):\n    \"\"\"Subscript index position: an `ast.Slice` -> `Slice(...)`, else the expr.\"\"\"\n    # Python <3.9 wrapped the index in ast.Index; unwrap if present.\n    inner = getattr(ast, \"Index\", None)\n    if inner is not None and isinstance(node, inner):\n        node = node.value\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Slice):\n        def bound(b):\n            # omitted bound (absent field) OR an explicit None constant -> NoBound.\n            # Python treats `a[None:5]` exactly like `a[:5]`; only a *value* None\n            # (Constant(None) outside a slice) becomes NoneVal. Keeping the two\n            # distinct lets NoneVal be a real value without perturbing slices.\n            if b is None or (isinstance(b, ast.Constant) and b.value is None):\n                return \"NoBound\"\n            return emit_expr(b)\n        return Ctor(\"Slice\", bound(node.lower), bound(node.upper), bound(node.step))\n    return emit_expr(node)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Statements\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef emit_stmt(node: ast.stmt):\n    if isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):\n        a = node.args\n        if a.vararg or a.kwarg or a.kwonlyargs or a.defaults or a.kw_defaults:\n            raise Unsupported(\"complex function signature (defaults/varargs/kwonly)\")\n        if node.decorator_list:\n            raise Unsupported(\"decorators\")\n        names = [p.arg for p in (a.posonlyargs + a.args)]  # type annotations ignored\n        params = Ctor(\"Params\", *(q(n) for n in names))\n        info = scope_info(node.name, node.lineno)\n        if info and (info[0] or info[1] or info[2]):\n            cells, frees, _ = info\n            return Ctor(\"FuncDef\", q(node.name), params,\n                        Ctor(\"CellVars\", *(q(n) for n in cells)),\n                        Ctor(\"FreeVars\", *(q(n) for n in frees)),\n                        emit_stmts(node.body))\n        return Ctor(\"FuncDef\", q(node.name), params, emit_stmts(node.body))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Return):\n        if node.value is None:\n            return Ctor(\"Return\")\n        return Ctor(\"Return\", emit_expr(node.value))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Assign):\n        if len(node.targets) != 1:\n            raise Unsupported(\"chained assignment (a = b = ...)\")\n        # Target is just an expression in store position (Name / Tuple / Subscript / Attribute).\n        return Ctor(\"Assign\", emit_expr(node.targets[0]), emit_expr(node.value))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.AugAssign):\n        op = _BINOP.get(type(node.op))\n        if op is None:\n            raise Unsupported(f\"AugAssign {type(node.op).__name__}\")\n        return Ctor(\"AugAssign\", emit_expr(node.target), q(op), emit_expr(node.value))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.If):\n        return Ctor(\"If\", emit_expr(node.test),\n                    emit_stmts(node.body), emit_stmts(node.orelse))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.For):\n        if node.orelse:\n            raise Unsupported(\"for-else\")\n        return Ctor(\"For\", emit_expr(node.target), emit_expr(node.iter),\n                    emit_stmts(node.body))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.While):\n        if node.orelse:\n            raise Unsupported(\"while-else\")\n        return Ctor(\"While\", emit_expr(node.test), emit_stmts(node.body))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Break):\n        return \"Break\"\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Continue):\n        return \"Continue\"\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Pass):\n        return \"Pass\"\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Assert):\n        if node.msg is not None:\n            return Ctor(\"Assert\", emit_expr(node.test), emit_expr(node.msg))\n        return Ctor(\"Assert\", emit_expr(node.test))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Expr):\n        return Ctor(\"Expr\", emit_expr(node.value))\n\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Import):\n        return Ctor(\"Import\", *(q(n.name) for n in node.names))\n    if isinstance(node, ast.ImportFrom):\n        mod = node.module if node.module is not None else \"\"\n        return Ctor(\"ImportFrom\", q(mod), *(q(n.name) for n in node.names))\n\n    raise Unsupported(f\"Stmt {type(node).__name__}\")\n\n\ndef emit_stmts(stmts) -> Seq:\n    return Seq([emit_stmt(s) for s in stmts])\n\n\ndef emit_module(tree: ast.Module) -> Ctor:\n    return Ctor(\"Module\", emit_stmts(tree.body))\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Lisp-style pretty printer\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWIDTH = 72  # an expression constructor wider than this breaks onto its own lines\n\n\ndef render(node, ind: int = 0) -> str:\n    if isinstance(node, str):\n        return node\n\n    if isinstance(node, Seq):\n        if not node.items:\n            return \"\"\n        pad = \"  \" * ind\n        return (\"\\n\" + pad).join(render(it, ind) for it in node.items)\n\n    # Ctor\n    cind = ind + 1\n    cpad = \"  \" * cind\n    has_block = any(isinstance(a, Seq) for a in node.args)\n\n    if not has_block:\n        inline = node.name + \"(\" + \", \".join(render(a, ind) for a in node.args) + \")\"\n        if \"\\n\" not in inline and len(\"  \" * ind) + len(inline) <= WIDTH:\n            return inline\n        parts = [render(a, cind) for a in node.args]\n        return node.name + \"(\\n\" + cpad + (\",\\n\" + cpad).join(parts) + \")\"\n\n    # Has a statement block: inline the args before the first block, then put\n    # each remaining arg (blocks included) on its own indented line.\n    first = next(i for i, a in enumerate(node.args) if isinstance(a, Seq))\n    head = node.args[:first]\n    rest = node.args[first:]\n    open_line = node.name + \"(\" + \", \".join(render(a, ind) for a in head) + (\",\" if head else \"\")\n    lines = []\n    for k, a in enumerate(rest):\n        comma = \",\" if k < len(rest) - 1 else \"\"\n        lines.append(cpad + render(a, cind) + comma)\n    return open_line + \"\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(lines) + \")\"\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# CLI\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        prog=\"py2mpy\",\n        description=\"Pure CPython-AST -> K-constructor transliterator.\",\n    )\n    ap.add_argument(\"file\", help=\"Python source file\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--ast\", action=\"store_true\",\n                    help=\"print the Python AST (ast.dump) instead of the .mpy term\")\n    args = ap.parse_args(argv)\n\n    with open(args.file, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        src = fh.read()\n    tree = ast.parse(src, filename=args.file)\n    _walk_symtable(symtable.symtable(src, args.file, \"exec\"))\n\n    if args.ast:\n        print(ast.dump(tree, indent=2))\n    else:\n        print(render(emit_module(tree)))\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n\n===== reference-semantics/semantics.k =====\n90 reference-semantics/semantics.k\n// reference/src/semantics.k — the supplied `.mpy` semantics, ASSEMBLED.\n//\n//   kompile reference-semantics/semantics.k --backend llvm \\\n//     --main-module MPY-KRUN --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\n//     --output-definition runtime-kompiled\n//\n// Concrete-only legs are included through MPY-KRUN. Proof definitions import\n// MPY and any explicit proof extensions supplied by the task author.\n//\n// Files (dependency order — everything imports MPY-CORE):\n//   semantics/syntax.k         MPY-SYNTAX       AST productions\n//   semantics/core.k           MPY-CORE         values, config, load, lookup, shared helpers   [required]\n//   semantics/iter.k           MPY-ITER         the iterator protocol (#iterNext/#iterYield/#iterDone) decl\n//   semantics/range.k          MPY-RANGE        rangeObj: inRange / rangeLen / its #iterNext\n//   semantics/operators.k      MPY-OPERATORS    operator DISPATCH (UnaryOp/BinOp/Compare/BoolOp) — domain files own the cases\n//   semantics/int.k            MPY-INT          Int operator cases; floored // and % (pyMod)\n//   semantics/bool.k           MPY-BOOL         Bool operator cases + truthy not/and/or (BoolOp)\n//   semantics/float.k          MPY-FLOAT        Float Val: literal + opaque float ops ([concrete] for krun)\n//   semantics/str.k            MPY-STR          str(IntSeq): literal, +, ==, in\n//   semantics/set.k            MPY-SET          set(str): distinct char codes (len / membership)\n//   semantics/list.k           MPY-LIST         ListExpr, list(ValSeq): +, ==, in\n//   semantics/tuple.k          MPY-TUPLE        TupleExpr, tuple ==, target-binding + unpacking\n//   semantics/subscript.k      MPY-SUBSCRIPT    Subscript indexing + Slice\n//   semantics/comprehension.k  MPY-COMPREHENSION  list comprehensions [ELT for T in ITER if COND]\n//   semantics/methods.k        MPY-METHODS      pure str methods: is*/lower/upper/swapcase/startswith\n//   semantics/controls.k       MPY-CONTROLS     Assign/AugAssign, If, IfExp, For, While, break/continue\n//   semantics/functions.k      MPY-FUNCTIONS    def / call / return (frame stack)\n//   semantics/builtins.k       MPY-BUILTINS     builtins as calls: len/sum/abs/min/max/ord/chr/str\n//   semantics/call.k           MPY-CALL         ALL Call routing + argument evaluation\n//   semantics/sort.k           MPY-SORT         trusted sorted(): opaque sortVS + concrete insertion sort\n//   semantics/assert.k         MPY-ASSERT       Assert oracle (smoke programs only)\n//   semantics/dict.k           MPY-DICT         order-preserving dictV(KS,VS) + .keys() + list()\n\nrequires \"semantics/syntax.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/core.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/iter.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/range.k\"\nrequires \"sem…11335 tokens truncated…dOpE(OO, O), appendIE(ON, CUR))\n       requires notBool inLevelE(L, O)\n  rule passLGoE(_:String, CUR:Int, oCons(_:String, _:OpSeq), .IntSeq, OO:OpSeq, ON:IntSeq)\n    => evp(OO, appendIE(ON, CUR)) [owise]\n  syntax Bool ::= inLevelE(String, String) [function, total]\n  rule inLevelE(\"mul\", O:String) => O ==String \"*\" orBool O ==String \"//\" orBool O ==String \"/\"\n  rule inLevelE(\"add\", O:String) => O ==String \"+\" orBool O ==String \"-\"\n  rule inLevelE(_:String, _:String) => false [owise]\n  syntax OpSeq ::= appendOpE(OpSeq, String) [function, total]\n  rule appendOpE(.OpSeq, O:String) => oCons(O, .OpSeq)\n  rule appendOpE(oCons(H:String, T:OpSeq), O:String) => oCons(H, appendOpE(T, O))\n  syntax IntSeq ::= appendIE(IntSeq, Int) [function, total]\n  rule appendIE(.IntSeq, N:Int) => iCons(N, .IntSeq)\n  rule appendIE(iCons(H:Int, T:IntSeq), N:Int) => iCons(H, appendIE(T, N))\n\n  // ==== hashlib.md5(S).hexdigest() — a trusted opaque digest ==================\n  // The md5 value itself is a named shared trust (sortVS-style, no concrete\n  // twin); proofs use the length/hex-charset lemmas (lemmas/builtins.k).\n  syntax KItem ::= \"#md5\"\n  rule <k> Call(Attribute(Name(\"hashlib\"), \"md5\"), (E:Expr, .Exprs)) => E ~> #md5 ... </k>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> str(CS:IntSeq) ~> #md5 => md5Obj(CS) ... </k>\n  syntax Val ::= md5Obj(IntSeq)\n  rule applyMethod(md5Obj(CS:IntSeq), \"hexdigest\", .Vals) => str(md5hexCodes(CS))\n  syntax IntSeq ::= md5hexCodes(IntSeq) [function, total, symbol(md5hexCodes), no-evaluators]\n\n  // ==== isinstance(V, int|str) — an ordinary 2-arg builtin ===================\n  // The type argument (int/str) is an ordinary name that resolves via the builtins frame to\n  // typeV (see core.k's config); no Call-level special case. isIntV/isStrV mirror the old\n  // owise-on-Val test (concrete-sort args decide; a symbolic Val stays owise, as before).\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"isinstance\", V:Val, typeV(\"int\"), .Vals) => isIntV(V)\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"isinstance\", V:Val, typeV(\"str\"), .Vals) => isStrV(V)\n  syntax Bool ::= isIntV(Val) [function] | isStrV(Val) [function]\n  rule isIntV(_:Int)         => true\n  rule isIntV(_:Val)         => false [owise]\n  rule isStrV(str(_:IntSeq)) => true\n  rule isStrV(_:Val)         => false [owise]\nendmodule\n\n===== reference-semantics/semantics/sort.k =====\n72 reference-semantics/semantics/sort.k\n// reference/src/semantics/sort.k — MPY-SORT\n// Trusted sorted(): sorted(list) as an opaque ascending permutation `sortVS` — opaque symbolic\n// for kprove (a trusted primitive, correctness/ordering proved in Lean / notes/sort-probe),\n// and a concrete insertion sort for krun. Wrappers verify the logic AROUND the sort (index /\n// slice / filter / dedup); indexing an opaque sortVS(VS) carries through as an opaque term, so\n// no ordering lemma is needed in K — the trust (sortVS = real sorted) is discharged by each\n// problem's differential test. Only int lists (compared by projectIntTotal); keyed / string\n// sorts are separate. Fires only via the \"sorted\" builtin, so inert for non-sorting programs.\n\nmodule MPY-SORT\n  imports MPY-BUILTINS\n  imports MPY-SUBSCRIPT\n\n  // sortVS(VS): the ascending sort of the Val list VS. Opaque for symbolic VS (no-evaluators);\n  // concrete insertion sort for krun.\n  // Concrete sort matches Int-sorted elements directly (an int Val IS an Int); projectIntTotal\n  // (lemmas-only) is not available in the semantics. Int and str lists.\n  syntax ValSeq ::= sortVS(ValSeq) [function, total, symbol(sortVS), no-evaluators]\n  syntax ValSeq ::= insVS(Int, ValSeq) [function]\n  rule sortVS(.ValSeq)                => .ValSeq          [concrete]\n  rule sortVS(vCons(X:Int, R:ValSeq)) => insVS(X, sortVS(R)) [concrete]\n  rule insVS(X:Int, .ValSeq)                => vCons(X, .ValSeq) [concrete]\n  rule insVS(X:Int, vCons(Y:Int, R:ValSeq)) => vCons(X, vCons(Y, R)) requires X <=Int Y [concrete]\n  rule insVS(X:Int, vCons(Y:Int, R:ValSeq)) => vCons(Y, insVS(X, R)) requires X  >Int Y [concrete]\n  // str elements insert by the shared lexicographic strLt (methods.k)\n  syntax ValSeq ::= insVSs(IntSeq, ValSeq) [function]\n  rule sortVS(vCons(str(CS:IntSeq), R:ValSeq)) => insVSs(CS, sortVS(R)) [concrete]\n  rule insVSs(A:IntSeq, .ValSeq) => vCons(str(A), .ValSeq) [concrete]\n  rule insVSs(A:IntSeq, vCons(str(B:IntSeq), R:ValSeq)) => vCons(str(A), vCons(str(B), R))\n       requires strLt(A, B) orBool A ==K B [concrete]\n  rule insVSs(A:IntSeq, vCons(str(B:IntSeq), R:ValSeq)) => vCons(str(B), insVSs(A, R))\n       requires notBool (strLt(A, B) orBool A ==K B) [concrete]\n\n  // sorted(list) constructs a NEW object: k-cell pre-dispatch (beats the [owise]\n  // applyBuiltin routing in call.k) so the result allocates.\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sorted\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), .Vals))\n        => #alloc(list(sortVS(VS))) ... </k>\n\n  // mutator: xs.sort() — the in-place heap write over the same trusted sortVS\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(ref(H:Int), \"sort\")), .Vals) => noneV ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> list(VS:ValSeq => sortVS(VS)) ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  // ==== keyed / reversed sorted() (WP2) =====================================\n  // sortKeyVS(VS, KV): the stable ascending sort of VS by the key value KV\n  // (a closure/builtin/type — anything callable). OPAQUE here; the concrete\n  // leg (MPY-CONCRETE, llvm only) computes keys by REAL calls and stable-\n  // inserts, at priority(40) over these.\n  syntax ValSeq ::= sortKeyVS(ValSeq, Val) [function, total, symbol(sortKeyVS), no-evaluators]\n\n  syntax ValSeq ::= revVS(ValSeq) [function, total]\n                  | revVSAcc(ValSeq, ValSeq) [function, total]\n  rule revVS(S:ValSeq) => revVSAcc(S, .ValSeq)\n  rule revVSAcc(.ValSeq, A:ValSeq) => A\n  rule revVSAcc(vCons(V:Val, R:ValSeq), A:ValSeq) => revVSAcc(R, vCons(V, A))\n\n  syntax ValSeq ::= condRev(ValSeq, Bool) [function, total]\n  rule condRev(S:ValSeq, false) => S\n  rule condRev(S:ValSeq, true)  => revVS(S)\n\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sorted\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), kwV(\"key\", KV:Val), .Vals))\n        => #alloc(list(sortKeyVS(VS, KV))) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sorted\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), kwV(\"key\", KV:Val), kwV(\"reverse\", RB:Bool), .Vals))\n        => #alloc(list(condRev(sortKeyVS(VS, KV), RB))) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sorted\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), kwV(\"reverse\", RB:Bool), .Vals))\n        => #alloc(list(condRev(sortVS(VS), RB))) ... </k>\n\n  // Indexing the opaque sorted list: `valSeqAt(sortVS(VS), I)` is DEFINED because valSeqAt is\n  // [total] (subscript.k) — it stays an abstract total value for a symbolic sort and reduces\n  // over the concrete sort for krun. No separate sortedAt indirection is needed; wrappers write\n  // their postcondition directly as valSeqAt(sortVS(VS), …).\nendmodule\n\n===== reference-semantics/semantics/set.k =====\n40 reference-semantics/semantics/set.k\n// reference/src/semantics/set.k — MPY-SET\n\nmodule MPY-SET\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  // a set value, carried as its distinct codes in first-seen order (order is irrelevant\n  // to membership/cardinality — the two observations sets support here).\n  syntax Val ::= setV(IntSeq)\n\n  // membership of a code in the accumulated distinct-code sequence\n  syntax Bool ::= codeIn(Int, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule codeIn(_:Int, .IntSeq)                => false\n  rule codeIn(C:Int, iCons(H:Int, T:IntSeq)) => C ==Int H orBool codeIn(C, T)\n\n  // the distinct codes of CS (insert-if-absent fold, first-seen order)\n  syntax IntSeq ::= dedupCodes(IntSeq)         [function, total]\n                  | dedupFrom(IntSeq, IntSeq)  [function, total]\n  rule dedupCodes(CS:IntSeq) => dedupFrom(CS, .IntSeq)\n  rule dedupFrom(.IntSeq, ACC:IntSeq) => ACC\n  rule dedupFrom(iCons(C:Int, S:IntSeq), ACC:IntSeq) => dedupFrom(S, ACC)\n       requires codeIn(C, ACC)\n  rule dedupFrom(iCons(C:Int, S:IntSeq), ACC:IntSeq) => dedupFrom(S, snocCode(ACC, C))\n       requires notBool codeIn(C, ACC)\n\n  syntax IntSeq ::= snocCode(IntSeq, Int) [function, total]\n  rule snocCode(.IntSeq, C:Int)                => iCons(C, .IntSeq)\n  rule snocCode(iCons(H:Int, T:IntSeq), C:Int) => iCons(H, snocCode(T, C))\n\n  // ==== set equality: two sets are equal iff mutually subsuming ==============\n  // subsetCodes(A, B) — every code of A occurs in B (duplicates in A are harmless).\n  syntax Bool ::= subsetCodes(IntSeq, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule subsetCodes(.IntSeq, _:IntSeq)                => true\n  rule subsetCodes(iCons(C:Int, S:IntSeq), B:IntSeq) => codeIn(C, B) andBool subsetCodes(S, B)\n\n  syntax Bool ::= sameSet(IntSeq, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule sameSet(A:IntSeq, B:IntSeq) => subsetCodes(A, B) andBool subsetCodes(B, A)\n\n  // set == set  (the only comparison sets support here)\n  rule applyCmp(\"==\", setV(A:IntSeq), setV(B:IntSeq)) => sameSet(A, B)\nendmodule\n\n===== reference-semantics/semantics/operators.k =====\n47 reference-semantics/semantics/operators.k\n// reference/src/semantics/operators.k — MPY-OPERATORS\n// The operator DISPATCH layer: operands evaluate by strictness (heat/cool over KResult=Val),\n// the cooled node hands to applyUn/applyBin/applyCmp (declared in core.k; each domain file\n// owns its cases — bool.k, int.k, float.k, str.k, list.k, tuple.k, set.k, dict.k).\n\nmodule MPY-OPERATORS\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-ITER\n\n  rule <k> UnaryOp(OP:String, V:Val) => applyUn(OP, V) ... </k>\n\n  rule <k> BinOp(OP:String, L:Val, R:Val) => applyBin(OP, L, R) ... </k>\n\n  // Compare's right operand sits under the CmpOp wrapper — contexts instead of attributes\n  context Compare(HOLE, _)\n  context Compare(_:Val, CmpOp(_, HOLE))\n  rule <k> Compare(LV:Val, CmpOp(OP:String, RV:Val)) => applyCmp(OP, LV, RV) ... </k> [owise]\n\n  rule applyCmp(\"is\",     V:Val, noneV) => V ==K noneV\n  rule applyCmp(\"is not\", V:Val, noneV) => notBool (V ==K noneV)\n\n  // ==== operand deref: heap objects combine/compare by STRUCTURE ============\n  // (Python: list == is structural; identity only via `is`.) priority(40)\n  // preempts the generic dispatch so applyUn/applyBin/applyCmp never see a ref.\n  rule <k> BinOp(OP:String, ref(H:Int), R:Expr) => BinOp(OP, V, R) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> BinOp(OP:String, L:Val, ref(H:Int)) => BinOp(OP, L, V) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool isRefV(L)\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  // the left operand of `in`/`not in` is an ELEMENT (compares by ==K) — never deref'd\n  rule <k> Compare(ref(H:Int), CmpOp(OP:String, R:Expr)) => Compare(V, CmpOp(OP, R)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires OP =/=String \"in\" andBool OP =/=String \"not in\"\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> Compare(L:Val, CmpOp(OP:String, ref(H:Int))) => Compare(L, CmpOp(OP, V)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool isRefV(L)\n        orBool OP ==String \"in\" orBool OP ==String \"not in\"\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  rule <k> UnaryOp(OP:String, ref(H:Int)) => UnaryOp(OP, V) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\nendmodule\n\n===== reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k =====\n109 reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k\n// reference/src/semantics/controls.k — MPY-CONTROLS\n\nmodule MPY-CONTROLS\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-TUPLE\n  imports MPY-ITER\n\n  // ==== Assign / AugAssign (write the current scope; RHS evaluated by strictness) ==\n  rule <k> Assign(Name(X:String), V:Val) => .K ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ X <- V ], _) ... </scopes>\n  rule <k> Assign(Name(X:String), V:Val) => #cellW({M[X]}:>Val, V) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       requires \"$cells\" in_keys(M)\n        andBool pnMember(X, cellsOf({M[\"$cells\"]}:>Val))\n        andBool X in_keys(M) andBool isCellRef({M[X]}:>Val)\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  rule <k> AugAssign(Name(X:String), OP:String, V:Val) => .K ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ X <- applyBin(OP, {M[X]}:>Val, V) ], _) ... </scopes>\n       requires X in_keys(M)\n  // `lst += [..]` where lst is a heap ref: the generic rule leaves applyBin(OP, ref, V) — but the\n  // ref-deref preemption is BinOp-level (operators.k), so applyBin never derefs and it sticks. Route\n  // the ref case through BinOp so the deref + list-concat + #alloc path fires (result is a fresh ref).\n  rule <k> AugAssign(Name(X:String), OP:String, V:Val) => Assign(Name(X), BinOp(OP, Name(X), V)) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       requires X in_keys(M) andBool isRefV({M[X]}:>Val)\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  // ==== import trivia: `from math import floor, ceil` binds the supported\n  // names as builtins in the current scope; every other import is a no-op\n  rule <k> ImportFrom(\"math\", NS:ParamNames) => #bindImports(NS) ... </k>\n  rule <k> ImportFrom(_:String, _:ParamNames) => .K ... </k> [owise]\n  syntax KItem ::= #bindImports(ParamNames)\n  rule <k> #bindImports(.ParamNames) => .K ... </k>\n  rule <k> #bindImports((N:String, NS:ParamNames)) => #bindImports(NS) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ N <- builtinV(N) ], _) ... </scopes>\n       requires N ==String \"floor\" orBool N ==String \"ceil\"\n  rule <k> #bindImports((N:String, NS:ParamNames)) => #bindImports(NS) ... </k>\n       requires notBool (N ==String \"floor\" orBool N ==String \"ceil\")\n\n  // ==== Expr statement: evaluate for effect, discard the value ===============\n  // (bare calls — mutator methods, docstrings; the WP0 statement-trivia rung)\n  rule <k> Expr(_:Val) => .K ... </k>\n\n  // ==== If (condition evaluated by strictness) ==============================\n  syntax KItem ::= #branch(Bool, Stmts, Stmts)\n  rule <k> If(C:Val, T:Stmts, E:Stmts) => #branch(truthy(C), T, E) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #branch(true,  T:Stmts, _:Stmts) => T ... </k>\n  rule <k> #branch(false, _:Stmts, E:Stmts) => E ... </k>\n\n  // ==== IfExp: ternary T if C else E ========================================\n  rule <k> IfExp(V:Val, T:Expr, _:Expr) => T ... </k>\n       requires truthy(V)\n  rule <k> IfExp(V:Val, _:Expr, E:Expr) => E ... </k>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n\n  // ==== For: one loop, in-cell continuation, over #iterNext =================\n  // (the iterable is evaluated once, by strictness; the protocol stays rewrites —\n  // circularities anchor on #loop and narrowing substitutes the structure)\n  syntax KItem ::= #loop(Val, Expr, Stmts) | #loopStep(Expr, Stmts)\n                 | #while(Expr, Stmts) | #whileCond(Expr, Stmts)\n                 | #loopLbl(K) | \"#cont\" | \"#brk\"\n\n  rule <k> For(T:Expr, OBJ:Val, B:Stmts) => #loop(OBJ, T, B) ... </k>\n\n  rule <k> #loop(IT:Iterable, T:Expr, B:Stmts) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #loopStep(T, B) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #loopStep(_:Expr, _:Stmts) => .K ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, REST:Iterable) ~> #loopStep(T:Expr, B:Stmts)\n        => #bindTgt(T, V) ~> B ~> #loopLbl(#loop(REST, T, B)) ... </k>\n\n  // ==== While ==============================================================\n  rule <k> While(C:Expr, B:Stmts) => #while(C, B) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #while(C:Expr, B:Stmts) => C ~> #whileCond(C, B) ... </k>\n  rule <k> V:Val ~> #whileCond(C:Expr, B:Stmts) => B ~> #loopLbl(#while(C, B)) ... </k>\n       requires truthy(V)\n  rule <k> V:Val ~> #whileCond(_C:Expr, _B:Stmts) => .K ... </k>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n\n  // ==== loop control (break / continue) =====================================\n  rule <k> #loopLbl(NEXT:K) => NEXT ... </k>\n  rule <k> Continue => #cont ... </k>\n  rule <k> Break => #brk ... </k>\n  rule <k> #cont ~> #loopLbl(NEXT:K) => NEXT ... </k>\n  rule <k> #cont ~> (_:KItem => .K) ... </k> [owise]\n  rule <k> #brk ~> #loopLbl(_:K) => .K ... </k>\n  rule <k> #brk ~> (_:KItem => .K) ... </k> [owise]\n\n  // ==== heap-object deref at the truthiness/iteration consumers ==============\n  // (priority(40) preempts the generic Val rules so truthy/#loop never see a ref)\n  rule <k> If(ref(H:Int), T:Stmts, E:Stmts) => If(V, T, E) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> IfExp(ref(H:Int), T:Expr, E:Expr) => IfExp(V, T, E) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> ref(H:Int) ~> #whileCond(C:Expr, B:Stmts) => V ~> #whileCond(C, B) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n  // For derefs its iterable ONCE at loop start (iteration is over the snapshot;\n  // mutating the iterated list inside its own loop is outside the subset)\n  rule <k> For(T:Expr, ref(H:Int), B:Stmts) => For(T, V, B) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\nendmodule\n\n===== reference-semantics/semantics/concrete.k =====\n60 reference-semantics/semantics/concrete.k\n// reference/src/semantics/concrete.k — MPY-CONCRETE\n// Concrete-execution-only rules, visible ONLY to the llvm build (run.sh kompiles\n// MPY-CONCRETE; verification builds import MPY and never see these). Rationale:\n// a k-cell rule matching value constructors NARROWS abstract Vals in the haskell\n// backend ([concrete] is not respected on rewrite rules) — probed on\n// 26-remove-duplicates, where element compares branched into deepEqVS.\n\nmodule MPY-CONCRETE\n  imports MPY\n\n  // deep equality for list compares whose elements are heap objects\n  // (list-of-lists): Python == is structural at every depth.\n  rule <k> Compare(list(A:ValSeq), CmpOp(\"==\", list(B:ValSeq))) => deepEqVS(A, B, HP) ... </k>\n       <heap> HP:Map </heap>\n       requires hasRefVS(A) orBool hasRefVS(B)\n  rule <k> Compare(list(A:ValSeq), CmpOp(\"!=\", list(B:ValSeq))) => notBool deepEqVS(A, B, HP) ... </k>\n       <heap> HP:Map </heap>\n       requires hasRefVS(A) orBool hasRefVS(B)\n\n  // ==== keyed sort, concrete leg ============================================\n  // Computes each key by a REAL call through the uniform #callee machinery\n  // (closures, len, type objects all work), stable-inserts on the key, and\n  // allocates the result. priority(40) beats sort.k's opaque rules, so krun\n  // runs this and proofs (which never see MPY-CONCRETE) keep sortKeyVS.\n  syntax Val ::= kvP(Val, Val)\n  syntax KItem ::= #ksort(ValSeq, Val, ValSeq, Bool)\n                 | #ksIns(Val, ValSeq, Val, ValSeq, Bool)\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sorted\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), kwV(\"key\", KV:Val), .Vals))\n        => #ksort(VS, KV, .ValSeq, false) ... </k>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sorted\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), kwV(\"key\", KV:Val), kwV(\"reverse\", RB:Bool), .Vals))\n        => #ksort(VS, KV, .ValSeq, RB) ... </k>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> #ksort(.ValSeq, _:Val, ACC:ValSeq, RB:Bool)\n        => #alloc(list(condRev(unpairVS(ACC), RB))) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #ksort(vCons(V:Val, R:ValSeq), KV:Val, ACC:ValSeq, RB:Bool)\n        => KV ~> #callee((V, .Exprs)) ~> #ksIns(V, R, KV, ACC, RB) ... </k>\n  rule <k> K:Val ~> #ksIns(V:Val, R:ValSeq, KV:Val, ACC:ValSeq, RB:Bool)\n        => #ksort(R, KV, insPair(ACC, K, V), RB) ... </k>\n       requires notBool isKwV(K)\n\n  syntax ValSeq ::= insPair(ValSeq, Val, Val) [function]\n  rule insPair(.ValSeq, K:Val, V:Val) => vCons(kvP(K, V), .ValSeq)\n  rule insPair(vCons(kvP(K2:Val, V2:Val), R:ValSeq), K:Val, V:Val)\n    => vCons(kvP(K, V), vCons(kvP(K2, V2), R))\n       requires kLt(K, K2)\n  rule insPair(vCons(kvP(K2:Val, V2:Val), R:ValSeq), K:Val, V:Val)\n    => vCons(kvP(K2, V2), insPair(R, K, V))\n       requires notBool kLt(K, K2)\n\n  syntax Bool ::= kLt(Val, Val) [function]\n  rule kLt(I1:Int, I2:Int)             => I1 <Int I2\n  rule kLt(F1:Float, F2:Float)         => F1 <Float F2\n  rule kLt(str(A:IntSeq), str(B:IntSeq)) => strLt(A, B)\n\n  syntax ValSeq ::= unpairVS(ValSeq) [function, total]\n  rule unpairVS(.ValSeq) => .ValSeq\n  rule unpairVS(vCons(kvP(_:Val, V:Val), R:ValSeq)) => vCons(V, unpairVS(R))\n  rule unpairVS(vCons(V:Val, R:ValSeq)) => vCons(V, unpairVS(R)) [owise]\nendmodule\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
34:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:34:21.131Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_088a97266d078c35016a644ad9fe808196bee1f5009de92f3e","status":"completed","call_id":"call_a0DHMkuZTpdrFCh33zC8E6WU","name":"exec","input":"const qs = [\n[\"sem-main\",\"sed -n '50,130p' reference-semantics/semantics.k\"],\n[\"core\",\"sed -n '1,420p' reference-semantics/semantics/core.k\"],\n[\"list\",\"sed -n '1,360p' reference-semantics/semantics/list.k\"],\n[\"iter\",\"sed -n '1,300p' reference-semantics/semantics/iter.k\"],\n[\"functions\",\"sed -n '1,360p' reference-semantics/semantics/functions.k\"],\n[\"call\",\"sed -n '1,420p' reference-semantics/semantics/call.k\"],\n[\"builtins-top\",\"sed -n '1,260p' reference-semantics/semantics/builtins.k\"],\n[\"comp\",\"sed -n '1,320p' reference-semantics/semantics/comprehension.k\"],\n[\"syntax\",\"sed -n '1,360p' reference-semantics/semantics/syntax.k\"]\n];\nfor (const [name,cmd] of qs) {\n  const r=await tools.exec_command({cmd,workdir:\"/workspace\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:20000});\n  text(`===== ${name} =====\\n${r.output}`);\n}\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
35:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:34:21.612Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_a0DHMkuZTpdrFCh33zC8E6WU","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 11397)\nTotal output lines: 910\n\n===== sem-main =====\nrequires \"semantics/functions.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/builtins.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/call.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/sort.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/assert.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/dict.k\"\nrequires \"semantics/concrete.k\"\n\nmodule MPY\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-ITER\n  imports MPY-RANGE\n  imports MPY-OPERATORS\n  imports MPY-INT\n  imports MPY-BOOL\n  imports MPY-FLOAT\n  imports MPY-STR\n  imports MPY-SET\n  imports MPY-LIST\n  imports MPY-TUPLE\n  imports MPY-SUBSCRIPT\n  imports MPY-COMPREHENSION\n  imports MPY-METHODS\n  imports MPY-CONTROLS\n  imports MPY-FUNCTIONS\n  imports MPY-BUILTINS\n  imports MPY-CALL\n  imports MPY-SORT\n  imports MPY-ASSERT\n  imports MPY-DICT\nendmodule\n\n// The krun (llvm) main module: MPY plus the concrete-only legs (keyed sort's\n// real key calls, deep list equality). Verification builds import MPY and\n// never see MPY-CONCRETE. The llvm kompile MUST use --main-module MPY-KRUN —\n// with plain MPY the concrete legs are silently absent (this was live for a\n// while: sorted-key stuck and comprehension asserted wrong under krun).\nmodule MPY-KRUN\n  imports MPY\n  imports MPY-CONCRETE\nendmodule\n\n===== core =====\n// reference/src/semantics/core.k — MPY-CORE\n\nmodule MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-SYNTAX\n  imports INT\n  imports BOOL\n  imports STRING\n  imports MAP\n  imports LIST\n  imports K-EQUAL\n\n  // ==== values, the algebraic lists, and the scope heap =====================\n  syntax IntSeq ::= \".IntSeq\" | iCons(Int, IntSeq)\n  syntax ValSeq ::= \".ValSeq\" | vCons(Val, ValSeq)\n  syntax Str    ::= str(IntSeq)\n\n  // the iterable values, grouped under one sort (typing only — dispatch is <k>-cell)\n  syntax Iterable ::= list(ValSeq)\n                    | tuple(ValSeq)\n                    | Str\n                    | rangeObj(Int, Int, Int)\n                    | zipObj(ValSeq, ValSeq)\n                    | zipObjS(IntSeq, IntSeq)\n\n  syntax Val      ::= Int\n                    | Bool\n                    | \"noneV\"\n                    | Iterable\n                    | ref(Int)          // a heap object: <heap> holds its list(VS)\n                    | cellRef(Int)      // a closure cell: <heap> holds cellV(V)\n                    | closureVal(ParamNames, Stmts, Int)\n                    | typeV(String)     // a type object (int/str), resolved from the builtins frame\n                    | builtinV(String)  // a builtin function, resolved like any name (LEGB fallthrough)\n                    | boundMethodV(Val, String)   // a cooled Attribute: obj.method\n\n  syntax Parent   ::= \"root\" | parent(Int)\n  syntax Scope    ::= scope(Map, Parent)\n  syntax KResult  ::= Val\n  syntax Expr     ::= Val   // cooling puts results back into expression holes\n  syntax Vals     ::= List{Val, \",\"}\n  syntax Exc      ::= \"NoExc\" | \"AssertionError\"\n  syntax RetState ::= \"noRet\" | retV(Val)\n\n  // ==== configuration =======================================================\n  // The builtins namespace is a real scope at reserved location -1 (the bottom of every\n  // chain; scopeLoc only allocates >=1, so -1 never collides). The module scope (loc 0)\n  // has it as parent, so an unbound name resolves there last — exactly LEGB. `int`/`str`\n  // resolve to their type objects; any local/global binding shadows them via normal lookup.\n  configuration\n    <k>       #loadAll($PGM:Module) </k>\n    <env>     0 </env>\n    <scopes>   0     |-> scope(.Map, parent(-1))\n              -1    |-> builtinsScope </scopes>\n    <scopeLoc> 1 </scopeLoc>\n    <heap>    .Map </heap>\n    <heapLoc> 0 </heapLoc>\n    <stack>   .List </stack>\n    <ret>     noRet </ret>\n    <exc>     NoExc </exc>\n    <exit-code exit=\"\"> 0 </exit-code>\n\n  // ==== heap allocation (constructed lists become objects) ==================\n  // Cons-form emission with a freshness guard (the heap-list-probe discipline:\n  // an update-form H[N <- _] never re-normalizes symbolically). heapLoc is\n  // monotonic — it does NOT wind back at #pop: returned lists escape by ref.\n  // A bare list(VS) Val stays legal (read-only inputs in claims flow unboxed);\n  // only CONSTRUCTORS in program syntax allocate.\n  syntax Bool ::= isRefV(Val) [function, total]\n  rule isRefV(ref(_:Int)) => true\n  rule isRefV(_:Val)      => false [owise]\n\n  // closure cells (Python-faithful capture): the heap holds cellV(V); a\n  // cellRef surfacing as the k-redex reads through (lookup is the only use —\n  // cellRefs never escape to user-visible values)\n  syntax HeapVal ::= cellV(Val)\n  syntax Bool ::= isCellRef(Val) [function, total]\n  rule isCellRef(cellRef(_:Int)) => true\n  rule isCellRef(_:Val)          => false [owise]\n  // k-top deref for cell-bound reads surfacing INSIDE the annotated frame\n  // (AugAssign's in-place read and friends). The \"$cells\" guard keeps this\n  // DECIDABLY inapplicable in plain frames — an unguarded rule lets the\n  // prover narrow abstract k-top values into cellRef junk (probed on\n  // 26-remove-duplicates). Cross-frame reads (a comprehension closure\n  // reading the enclosing function's cellvar) deref inside #look instead.\n  rule <k> cellRef(H:Int) => V ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       <heap> ... H |-> cellV(V:Val) ... </heap>\n       requires \"$cells\" in_keys(M)\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  // write through a cell (Assign / #bindP / #bindTgt dispatch here on\n  // cell-bound names)\n  // a keyword argument cools to a TAGGED value (consumed by kw-aware builtins)\n  syntax Val ::= kwV(String, Val)\n  syntax KItem ::= #kwTag(String)\n  rule <k> KwArg(N:String, E:Expr) => E ~> #kwTag(N) ... </k>\n  rule <k> V:Val ~> #kwTag(N:String) => kwV(N, V) ... </k>\n       requires notBool isKwV(V)\n  syntax Bool ::= isKwV(Val) [function, total]\n  rule isKwV(kwV(_:String, _:Val)) => true\n  rule isKwV(_:Val)                => false [owise]\n\n  // the frame marker carries the CONCRETE cellvar list, so cell-write dispatch\n  // decides by pnMember even over an abstract frame rest (no prover branching)\n  syntax Val ::= cellsMark(ParamNames)\n  syntax ParamNames ::= cellsOf(Val) [function]\n  rule cellsOf(cellsMark(CVS:ParamNames)) => CVS\n  syntax Bool ::= pnMember(String, ParamNames) [function, total]\n  rule pnMember(_:String, .ParamNames) => false\n  rule pnMember(X:String, (P:String, R:ParamNames)) => X ==String P orBool pnMember(X, R)\n\n  syntax KItem ::= #cellW(Val, Val)\n  rule <k> #cellW(cellRef(H:Int), V:Val) => .K ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> cellV(_:Val => V) ... </heap>\n\n  syntax KItem ::= #alloc(Val)\n  rule <k> #alloc(V:Val) => ref(N) ... </k>\n       <heap>    H:Map => (N |-> V) H </heap>\n       <heapLoc> N:Int => N +Int 1 </heapLoc>\n       requires notBool N in_keys(H)\n\n  // ==== module load + statement sequencing ==================================\n  syntax KItem ::= #loadAll(Module)\n  rule <k> #loadAll(Module(SS:Stmts)) => SS ... </k>\n  rule <k> (S:Stmt SS:Stmts):Stmts => S ~> SS ... </k>\n  rule <k> .Stmts => .K ... </k>\n\n  // ==== Name lookup (walk the scope chain; builtins live in the -1 scope) ====\n  syntax KItem ::= #look(String, Int)\n  rule <k> Name(X:String) => #look(X, L) ... </k> <env> L:Int </env>\n  rule <k> #look(X:String, L:Int) => {M[X]}:>Val ... </k>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _:Parent) ... </scopes>\n       requires X in_keys(M)\n  // a SYNTACTICALLY cell-bound name reads through the heap cell AT THE\n  // LOOKUP (higher priority beats the plain return above on concrete cell\n  // bindings; abstract claim values take the plain rule unchanged) — this\n  // covers cross-frame cell reads (a comprehension closure reading the\n  // enclosing function's cellvar) without a narrowing-prone k-top redex\n  // guarded on the FOUND frame's DECLARED cellvars (pnMember over the\n  // cellsMark): decidable for every concrete frame pin — plain frames and\n  // non-cell names prune outright, so an abstract looked-up value never\n  // drags a narrowing cellV heap match along (probed on 5-intersperse and\n  // Q4's abstract `numbers` in the annotated frame)\n  rule <k> #look(X:String, L:Int) => V ... </k>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _:Parent) ... </scopes>\n       <heap> ... H |-> cellV(V:Val) ... </heap>\n       requires X in_keys(M) andBool \"$cells\" in_keys(M)\n        andBool pnMember(X, cellsOf({M[\"$cells\"]}:>Val))\n        andBool {M[X]}:>Val ==K cellRef(H)\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> #look(X:String, L:Int) => #look(X, P) ... </k>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, parent(P:Int)) ... </scopes>\n       requires notBool (X in_keys(M))\n\n  // the ONE predefined builtins scope (the -1 frame; claims write `-1 |-> builtinsScope`)\n  syntax Scope ::= \"builtinsScope\" [function, total]\n  rule builtinsScope\n    => scope(.Map [ \"len\"    <- builtinV(\"len\")    ]\n                  [ \"set\"    <- builtinV(\"set\")    ]\n                  [ \"sum\"    <- builtinV(\"sum\")    ]\n                  [ \"abs\"    <- builtinV(\"abs\")    ]\n                  [ \"min\"    <- builtinV(\"min\")    ]\n                  [ \"max\"    <- builtinV(\"max\")    ]\n                  [ \"ord\"    <- builtinV(\"ord\")    ]\n                  [ \"chr\"    <- builtinV(\"chr\")    ]\n                  [ \"range\"  <- builtinV(\"range\")  ]\n                  [ \"all\"    <- builtinV(\"all\")    ]\n                  [ \"any\"    <- builtinV(\"any\")    ]\n                  [ \"zip\"    <- builtinV(\"zip\")    ]\n                  [ \"isinstance\" <- builtinV(\"isinstance\") ]\n                  [ \"sorted\" <- builtinV(\"sorted\") ]\n                  [ \"list\"   <- builtinV(\"list\")   ]\n                  [ \"round\"  <- builtinV(\"round\")  ]\n                  [ \"bin\"    <- builtinV(\"bin\")    ]\n                  [ \"enumerate\" <- builtinV(\"enumerate\") ]\n                  [ \"map\"    <- builtinV(\"map\")    ]\n                  [ \"eval\"   <- builtinV(\"eval\")   ]\n                  [ \"int\"    <- typeV(\"int\")       ]\n                  [ \"str\"    <- typeV(\"str\")       ]\n                  [ \"float\"  <- typeV(\"float\")     ], root)\n\n  // ==== argument/element evaluation: ONE left-to-right loop, tagged by destination ==\n  // (list/tuple literals and calls all use it; modules extend ApplyK with their tags)\n  syntax ApplyK ::= toCall(Val)\n  syntax KItem  ::= #evalArgs(Exprs, Vals, ApplyK)\n                  | #evalArgCont(Exprs, Vals, ApplyK)\n                  | #applyK(ApplyK, Vals)\n  rule <k> #evalArgs((A:Expr, REST:Exprs), ACC:Vals, K:ApplyK) => A ~> #evalArgCont(REST, ACC, K) ... </k>\n  rule <k> V:Val ~> #evalArgCont(REST:Exprs, ACC:Vals, K:ApplyK) => #evalArgs(REST, appendVal(ACC, V), K) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #evalArgs(.Exprs, ACC:Vals, K:ApplyK) => #applyK(K, ACC) ... </k>\n\n  // ==== Int / Bool / None literals ==========================================\n  rule <k> Int(I:Int)   => I ... </k>\n  rule <k> Bool(B:Bool) => B ... </k>\n  rule <k> NoneVal      => noneV ... </k>\n\n  // ==== truthy (If, Assert, BoolOp, IfExp, not, While) ======================\n  syntax Bool ::= truthy(Val) [function]\n  rule truthy(B:Bool)          => B\n  rule truthy(noneV)           => false\n  rule truthy(I:Int)           => I =/=Int 0\n  rule truthy(str(S:IntSeq))   => notBool (S ==K .IntSeq)\n  rule truthy(list(V:ValSeq))  => notBool (V ==K .ValSeq)\n  rule truthy(tuple(V:ValSeq)) => notBool (V ==K .ValSeq)\n\n  // ==== extensible operator dispatch (cases added by the construct modules) ==\n  syntax Val  ::= applyUn(String, Val) [function]\n  syntax Val  ::= applyBin(String, Val, Val) [function]\n  syntax Bool ::= applyCmp(String, Val, Val) [function]\n\n  // ==== shared list helpers =================================================\n  syntax Vals ::= appendVal(Vals, Val) [function, total]\n  rule appendVal(.Vals, V:Val)              => V , .Vals\n  rule appendVal((V0:Val, VS:Vals), V:Val)  => V0 , appendVal(VS, V)\n\n  syntax ValSeq ::= vals2valSeq(Vals) [function, total]\n  rule vals2valSeq(.Vals)            => .ValSeq\n  rule vals2valSeq((V:Val, VS:Vals)) => vCons(V, vals2valSeq(VS))\n\n  // ==== shared sequence length (len / summaries across many modules) ========\n  // (positional access valSeqAt/intSeqAt + normIdx live in subscript.k)\n  syntax Int ::= vsLen(ValSeq) [function, total]\n  rule vsLen(.ValSeq)                => 0\n  rule vsLen(vCons(_:Val, S:ValSeq)) => 1 +Int vsLen(S)\n\n  syntax Int ::= isLen(IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule isLen(.IntSeq)                => 0\n  rule isLen(iCons(_:Int, S:IntSeq)) => 1 +Int isLen(S)\n\n  // in-bounds positional write (list subscript-assign); OOB leaves the seq unchanged\n  // (valid programs write in-bounds — mirrors valSeqAt's total-but-underspecified stance)\n  syntax ValSeq ::= setVSAt(ValSeq, Int, Val) [function, total]\n  rule setVSAt(.ValSeq, _:Int, _:Val)               => .ValSeq\n  rule setVSAt(vCons(_:Val, S:ValSeq), 0, V:Val)    => vCons(V, S)\n  rule setVSAt(vCons(W:Val, S:ValSeq), I:Int, V:Val) => vCons(W, setVSAt(S, I -Int 1, V))\n       requires I >Int 0\n  rule setVSAt(VS:ValSeq, I:Int, _:Val)             => VS\n       requires I <Int 0\nendmodule\n\n===== list =====\n// reference/src/semantics/list.k — MPY-LIST\n\nmodule MPY-LIST\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-ITER\n  imports MPY-OPERATORS\n\n  // ==== iteration (the iterator protocol's list case) =======================\n  rule <k> #iterNext(list(.ValSeq))                => #iterDone ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterNext(list(vCons(V:Val, R:ValSeq))) => #iterYield(V, list(R)) ... </k>\n\n  // ==== ListExpr: [...] literal -> a fresh heap object =======================\n  syntax ApplyK ::= \"toList\"\n  rule <k> ListExpr(ES:Exprs) => #evalArgs(ES, .Vals, toList) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toList, ACC:Vals) => #alloc(list(vals2valSeq(ACC))) ... </k>\n\n  // ==== list ops: + / == / != ===============================================\n  syntax ValSeq ::= valSeqConcat(ValSeq, ValSeq) [function, total]\n  rule valSeqConcat(.ValSeq, T:ValSeq)                => T\n  rule valSeqConcat(vCons(V:Val, S:ValSeq), T:ValSeq) => vCons(V, valSeqConcat(S, T))\n\n  // list + list constructs a NEW object (k-cell — it allocates; operands land here\n  // already deref'd). priority(45) beats the generic BinOp dispatch.\n  rule <k> BinOp(\"+\", list(A:ValSeq), list(B:ValSeq)) => #alloc(list(valSeqConcat(A, B))) ... </k>\n       [priority(45)]\n\n  rule applyCmp(\"==\", list(A:ValSeq), list(B:ValSeq)) => A ==K B\n  rule applyCmp(\"!=\", list(A:ValSeq), list(B:ValSeq)) => notBool (A ==K B)\n\n  // ==== deep equality when elements are heap objects (list-of-lists) ========\n  // Python == is structural at every depth. Fires ONLY when a ref is present\n  // (the guard decides on concrete seqs); the plain ==K path above is unchanged.\n  syntax Bool ::= hasRefVS(ValSeq) [function, total]\n  rule hasRefVS(.ValSeq)                => false\n  rule hasRefVS(vCons(V:Val, R:ValSeq)) => isRefV(V) orBool hasRefVS(R)\n\n  syntax Bool ::= deepEqVS(ValSeq, ValSeq, Map) [function]\n                | deepEqV(Val, Val, Map)        [function]\n  rule deepEqVS(.ValSeq, .ValSeq, _:Map)                   => true\n  rule deepEqVS(.ValSeq, vCons(_:Val, _:ValSeq), _:Map)    => false\n  rule deepEqVS(vCons(_:Val, _:ValSeq), .ValSeq, _:Map)    => false\n  rule deepEqVS(vCons(A:Val, As:ValSeq), vCons(B:Val, Bs:ValSeq), HP:Map)\n    => deepEqV(A, B, HP) andBool deepEqVS(As, Bs, HP)\n\n  rule deepEqV(ref(H:Int), B:Val, HP:Map) => deepEqV({HP[H]}:>Val, B, HP)\n       requires H in_keys(HP)\n  rule deepEqV(A:Val, ref(H:Int), HP:Map) => deepEqV(A, {HP[H]}:>Val, HP)\n       requires notBool isRefV(A) andBool H in_keys(HP)\n  rule deepEqV(list(A:ValSeq), list(B:ValSeq), HP:Map) => deepEqVS(A, B, HP)\n  rule deepEqV(A:Val, B:Val, _:Map) => A ==K B [owise]\n\n  // ==== mutator: xs.append(v) — an in-place heap write ======================\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(ref(H:Int), \"append\")), (V:Val, .Vals)) => noneV ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> list(VS:ValSeq => valSeqConcat(VS, vCons(V, .ValSeq))) ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  // ==== `x in list` — a <k>-cell fold over #iterNext ========================\n  syntax KItem ::= #memberAcc(Val, Iterable) | #memberCont(Val) | \"#notB\"\n  rule <k> Compare(LV:Val, CmpOp(\"in\",     list(VS:ValSeq))) => #memberAcc(LV, list(VS)) ... </k>\n  rule <k> Compare(LV:Val, CmpOp(\"not in\", list(VS:ValSeq))) => #memberAcc(LV, list(VS)) ~> #notB ... </k>\n  rule <k> #memberAcc(V:Val, IT:Iterable) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #memberCont(V) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #memberCont(_V:Val) => false ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(E:Val, _:Iterable) ~> #memberCont(V:Val) => true ... </k>\n       requires E ==K V\n  rule <k> #iterYield(E:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #memberCont(V:Val) => #memberAcc(V, R) ... </k>\n       requires notBool (E ==K V)\n  rule <k> B:Bool ~> #notB => notBool B ... </k>\nendmodule\n\n===== iter =====\n// reference/src/semantics/iter.k — MPY-ITER\n// The iterator protocol, DECLARATION ONLY. Each iterable's #iterNext case lives in that\n// iterable's own module (list/tuple/str in their modules, rangeObj in range.k, zip in\n// builtins.k). Consumers (the for-loop, `x in _`, sum/all/any/max/min) import this.\n\nmodule MPY-ITER\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  syntax KItem ::= #iterNext(Iterable) | \"#iterDone\" | #iterYield(Val, Iterable)\nendmodule\n\n===== functions =====\n// reference/src/semantics/functions.k — MPY-FUNCTIONS\n\nmodule MPY-FUNCTIONS\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  // call routing + callee/arg evaluation (#callee/#args/#argCont) live in call.k;\n  // this module owns the frame lifecycle (bind params, return, pop).\n  syntax KItem ::= frame(continuation: K, callerEnv: Int, savedLoc: Int)\n                 | #bindP(ParamNames, Vals)\n                 | \"#pop\"\n                 | \"#endcall\"\n\n  // ==== def / anonymous closure =============================================\n  rule <k> FuncDef(F:String, Params(PNS:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts) => .K ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ F <- closureVal(PNS, BODY, L) ], _) ... </scopes>\n\n  syntax Expr ::= closureExpr(ParamNames, Stmts)\n  rule <k> closureExpr(PNS:ParamNames, BODY:Stmts) => closureVal(PNS, BODY, L) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n\n  // ==== annotated def/lambda (closure cells; spec 2.3) ======================\n  // closureValC(params, cellvars, body, captured-cells). No frame anchor: all\n  // enclosing-local reads are freevars (symtable-complete) and go through the\n  // captured cells; everything else is global/builtin, so the callee frame's\n  // parent is the module scope (0) — sound after the defining frame dies.\n  syntax Val ::= closureValC(ParamNames, ParamNames, Stmts, Map)\n\n  // capture: resolve each freevar to the enclosing frame's cellRef, then bind\n  // (FuncDef) or yield (Lambda) the closure value.\n  syntax KItem ::= #mkClosure(String, ParamNames, ParamNames, ParamNames, Stmts, Map)\n                 | #mkLambda(ParamNames, ParamNames, ParamNames, Stmts, Map)\n  rule <k> FuncDef(F:String, Params(PNS:ParamNames), CellVars(CVS:ParamNames),\n                   FreeVars(FVS:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts)\n        => #mkClosure(F, PNS, CVS, FVS, BODY, .Map) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #mkClosure(F:String, PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames,\n                      (FV:String, FVR:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)\n        => #mkClosure(F, PNS, CVS, FVR, BODY, CM [ FV <- {M[FV]}:>Val ]) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       requires FV in_keys(M)\n  rule <k> #mkClosure(F:String, PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames,\n                      .ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, CM:Map) => .K ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ F <- closureValC(PNS, CVS, BODY, CM) ], _) ... </scopes>\n\n  rule <k> Lambda(Params(PNS:ParamNames), E:Expr)\n        => closureVal(PNS, Return(E) .Stmts, L) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n  rule <k> Lambda(Params(PNS:ParamNames), CellVars(CVS:ParamNames),\n                  FreeVars(FVS:ParamNames), E:Expr)\n        => #mkLambda(PNS, CVS, FVS, Return(E) .Stmts, .Map) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #mkLambda(PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames,\n                     (FV:String, FVR:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)\n        => #mkLambda(PNS, CVS, FVR, BODY, CM [ FV <- {M[FV]}:>Val ]) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ..…1397 tokens truncated…\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(closureVal(PNS:ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, DEFL:Int)), ACC:Vals) ~> CONT\n        => #bindP(PNS, ACC) ~> BODY ~> #endcall </k>\n       <env>     CALLERL:Int => NEWL </env>\n       <scopes>   STORE:Map => STORE [ NEWL <- scope(.Map, parent(DEFL)) ] </scopes>\n       <scopeLoc> NEWL:Int => NEWL +Int 1 </scopeLoc>\n       <stack>   .List => ListItem(frame(CONT, CALLERL, NEWL)) ... </stack>\n\n  // annotated closure: the frame starts with the captured freevar cells, its\n  // parent is the module scope (all enclosing-local reads go through cells),\n  // and the cellvars' fresh cells allocate before params bind (a cellvar param\n  // then writes through its cell in #bindP).\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(closureValC(PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)), ACC:Vals) ~> CONT\n        => #allocCells(CVS) ~> #bindP(PNS, ACC) ~> BODY ~> #endcall </k>\n       <env>     CALLERL:Int => NEWL </env>\n       <scopes>   STORE:Map => STORE [ NEWL <- scope(CM [ \"$cells\" <- cellsMark(CVS) ], parent(0)) ] </scopes>\n       <scopeLoc> NEWL:Int => NEWL +Int 1 </scopeLoc>\n       <stack>   .List => ListItem(frame(CONT, CALLERL, NEWL)) ... </stack>\n\n  syntax KItem ::= #allocCells(ParamNames)\n  rule <k> #allocCells(.ParamNames) => .K ... </k>\n  rule <k> #allocCells((CV:String, R:ParamNames)) => #allocCells(R) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ CV <- cellRef(N) ], _) ... </scopes>\n       <heap>    H:Map => (N |-> cellV(noneV)) H </heap>\n       <heapLoc> N:Int => N +Int 1 </heapLoc>\n       requires notBool N in_keys(H)\nendmodule\n\n===== builtins-top =====\n// reference/src/semantics/builtins.k — MPY-BUILTINS\n\nmodule MPY-BUILTINS\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-STR\n  imports MPY-SET\n  imports MPY-ITER\n  imports MPY-RANGE\n  imports MPY-INT\n  imports MPY-METHODS\n\n  // the builtins REGISTRY is core.k's builtinsScope (the -1 frame); names resolve by lookup\n\n  // Call routing + argument evaluation live in call.k, which also routes the fold\n  // builtins (sum/all/any/max/min) to the #_Acc folds below and everything else to\n  // applyBuiltin. This module owns applyBuiltin + the fold implementations.\n  syntax Val ::= applyBuiltin(String, Vals) [function]\n\n  // ==== len(obj) — O(1) per kind ============================================\n  syntax Int ::= seqLen(Val) [function]\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"len\", OBJ:Val, .Vals) => seqLen(OBJ)\n  rule seqLen(list(VS:ValSeq))                  => vsLen(VS)\n  rule seqLen(tuple(VS:ValSeq))                 => vsLen(VS)\n  rule seqLen(str(IS:IntSeq))                   => isLen(IS)\n  rule seqLen(setV(DS:IntSeq))                  => isLen(DS)\n  rule seqLen(rangeObj(LO:Int, HI:Int, ST:Int)) => rangeLen(LO, HI, ST)\n\n  // ==== list(seq) — materialize a list/tuple into a list (e.g. list(d.keys())) ==\n  // Minimal: the \"copy a sequence\" case (list of a list is itself; list of a tuple keeps order).\n  // list() of other iterables (str/range/set/zip) is added via the iterator fold when needed.\n  // (k-cell — list() constructs a NEW object)\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"list\")), (list(VS:ValSeq),  .Vals)) => #alloc(list(VS)) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"list\")), (tuple(VS:ValSeq), .Vals)) => #alloc(list(VS)) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"list\")), .Vals)                     => #alloc(list(.ValSeq)) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"list\")), (str(CS:IntSeq), .Vals))   => #alloc(list(charsOf(CS))) ... </k>\n  syntax ValSeq ::= charsOf(IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule charsOf(.IntSeq)                => .ValSeq\n  rule charsOf(iCons(C:Int, R:IntSeq)) => vCons(str(iCons(C, .IntSeq)), charsOf(R))\n\n  // ==== set(str) — distinct character codes =================================\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"set\", str(CS:IntSeq), .Vals) => setV(dedupCodes(CS))\n\n  // ==== abs(int) ============================================================\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"abs\", I:Int, .Vals) => absInt(I)\n\n  // ==== sum(iterable) — one #iterNext fold; intOf = int value of an int/bool ==\n  syntax KItem ::= #sumAcc(Iterable, Int) | #sumCont(Int)\n  rule <k> #sumAcc(IT:Iterable, ACC:Int) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #sumCont(ACC) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #sumCont(ACC:Int) => ACC ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #sumCont(ACC:Int)\n        => #sumAcc(R, ACC +Int intOf(V)) ... </k>\n       requires isInt(V) orBool isBool(V)\n\n  syntax Int ::= intOf(Val) [function]\n  rule intOf(I:Int)  => I\n  rule intOf(B:Bool) => #if B #then 1 #else 0 #fi\n\n  // ==== all / any (short-circuiting #iterNext folds) ========================\n  syntax KItem ::= #allAcc(Iterable) | \"#allCont\"\n  rule <k> #allAcc(IT:Iterable) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #allCont ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #allCont => true ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #allCont => #allAcc(R) ... </k>\n       requires truthy(V)\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, _:Iterable) ~> #allCont => false ... </k>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n\n  syntax KItem ::= #anyAcc(Iterable) | \"#anyCont\"\n  rule <k> #anyAcc(IT:Iterable) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #anyCont ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #anyCont => false ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, _:Iterable) ~> #anyCont => true ... </k>\n       requires truthy(V)\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #anyCont => #anyAcc(R) ... </k>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n\n  // ==== max / min over an iterable (#iterNext folds; first element seeds) ====\n  syntax KItem ::= #maxAcc0(Iterable) | \"#maxCont0\" | #maxAcc(Iterable, Int) | #maxCont(Int)\n  rule <k> #maxAcc0(IT:Iterable) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #maxCont0 ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #maxCont0 => #maxAcc(R, {V}:>Int) ... </k>\n       requires isInt(V)\n  rule <k> #maxAcc(IT:Iterable, M:Int) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #maxCont(M) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #maxCont(M:Int) => M ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #maxCont(M:Int)\n        => #maxAcc(R, maxInt(M, {V}:>Int)) ... </k>\n       requires isInt(V)\n\n  syntax KItem ::= #minAcc0(Iterable) | \"#minCont0\" | #minAcc(Iterable, Int) | #minCont(Int)\n  rule <k> #minAcc0(IT:Iterable) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #minCont0 ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #minCont0 => #minAcc(R, {V}:>Int) ... </k>\n       requires isInt(V)\n  rule <k> #minAcc(IT:Iterable, M:Int) => #iterNext(IT) ~> #minCont(M) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterDone ~> #minCont(M:Int) => M ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterYield(V:Val, R:Iterable) ~> #minCont(M:Int)\n        => #minAcc(R, minInt(M, {V}:>Int)) ... </k>\n       requires isInt(V)\n\n  // ==== variadic max / min (a Vals fold) ====================================\n  syntax Int ::= maxVals(Int, Vals) [function]\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"max\", I:Int, REST:Vals) => maxVals(I, REST)\n  rule maxVals(M:Int, .Vals)           => M\n  rule maxVals(M:Int, (I:Int, R:Vals)) => maxVals(maxInt(M, I), R)\n\n  syntax Int ::= minVals(Int, Vals) [function]\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"min\", I:Int, REST:Vals) => minVals(I, REST)\n  rule minVals(M:Int, .Vals)           => M\n  rule minVals(M:Int, (I:Int, R:Vals)) => minVals(minInt(M, I), R)\n\n  // ==== bin(n) — \"0b\" + binary digit codes (promoted from 103's defined fold) ==\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"bin\", N:Int, .Vals) => str(iCons(48, iCons(98, binCodes(N))))\n       requires N >=Int 0\n  // negative operand: the '-' sign prefixes the magnitude's digits\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"bin\", N:Int, .Vals)\n    => str(iCons(45, iCons(48, iCons(98, binCodes(0 -Int N)))))\n       requires N <Int 0\n  syntax IntSeq ::= binCodes(Int) [function, total]\n  rule binCodes(0) => iCons(48, .IntSeq)\n  rule binCodes(N:Int) => binAcc(N, .IntSeq) requires N >Int 0\n  syntax IntSeq ::= binAcc(Int, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule binAcc(0, ACC:IntSeq) => ACC\n  rule binAcc(N:Int, ACC:IntSeq)\n    => binAcc((N -Int pyMod(N, 2)) /Int 2, iCons(48 +Int pyMod(N, 2), ACC))\n       requires N >Int 0\n\n  // ==== enumerate(xs) — pairs (i, v); materialized eagerly over a list =========\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"enumerate\")), (list(VS:ValSeq), .Vals))\n        => #alloc(list(enumVS(VS, 0))) ... </k>\n  syntax ValSeq ::= enumVS(ValSeq, Int) [function, total]\n  rule enumVS(.ValSeq, _:Int) => .ValSeq\n  rule enumVS(vCons(V:Val, R:ValSeq), I:Int)\n    => vCons(tuple(vCons(I, vCons(V, .ValSeq))), enumVS(R, I +Int 1))\n\n  // ==== map(str, xs) — eager (only the str case is in the subset) =============\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"map\")), (typeV(\"str\"), list(VS:ValSeq), .Vals))\n        => #alloc(list(mapStrVS(VS))) ... </k>\n  syntax ValSeq ::= mapStrVS(ValSeq) [function, total]\n  rule mapStrVS(.ValSeq) => .ValSeq\n  rule mapStrVS(vCons(I:Int, R:ValSeq)) => vCons(str(strToCodes(Int2String(I))), mapStrVS(R))\n  rule mapStrVS(vCons(str(CS:IntSeq), R:ValSeq)) => vCons(str(CS), mapStrVS(R))\n\n  // ==== int(x) identities (int(round(x)) composes through) ====================\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"int\", I:Int, .Vals) => I\n\n  // ==== ord / chr ===========================================================\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"ord\", str(iCons(C:Int, .IntSeq)), .Vals) => C\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"chr\", I:Int, .Vals) => str(iCons(I, .IntSeq))\n       requires 0 <=Int I andBool I <Int 128\n\n  // ==== str(int) / str(str) =================================================\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"str\", I:Int, .Vals)       => str(strToCodes(Int2String(I)))\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"str\", str(CS:IntSeq), .Vals) => str(CS)\n\n  // ==== int(str) — a single digit char ('0'..'9', code 48..57) to its value =====\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"int\", str(iCons(C:Int, .IntSeq)), .Vals) => C -Int 48\n       requires 48 <=Int C andBool C <=Int 57\n\n  // ==== int(str) — a multi-digit non-negative numeral (Horner fold; the single-char rule covers len 1)\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"int\", str(CS:IntSeq), .Vals) => intDigAcc(CS, 0)\n       requires isLen(CS) >=Int 2\n  syntax Int ::= intDigAcc(IntSeq, Int) [function, total]\n  rule intDigAcc(.IntSeq, ACC:Int)             => ACC\n  rule intDigAcc(iCons(C:Int, R:IntSeq), ACC:Int) => intDigAcc(R, (ACC *Int 10) +Int (C -Int 48))\n\n  // ==== zip(a, b) — parallel iterable of pairs, truncating to the shorter =====\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"zip\", list(A:ValSeq), list(B:ValSeq), .Vals) => zipObj(A, B)\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"zip\", str(A:IntSeq), str(B:IntSeq), .Vals)   => zipObjS(A, B)\n\n  // zip's iterator-protocol cases (zip is created here, so its #iterNext lives here)\n  rule <k> #iterNext(zipObj(vCons(A:Val, As:ValSeq), vCons(B:Val, Bs:ValSeq)))\n        => #iterYield(tuple(vCons(A, vCons(B, .ValSeq))), zipObj(As, Bs)) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterNext(zipObj(.ValSeq, _:ValSeq))               => #iterDone ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterNext(zipObj(vCons(_:Val, _:ValSeq), .ValSeq)) => #iterDone ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterNext(zipObjS(iCons(A:Int, As:IntSeq), iCons(B:Int, Bs:IntSeq)))\n        => #iterYield(tuple(vCons(str(iCons(A, .IntSeq)), vCons(str(iCons(B, .IntSeq)), .ValSeq))), zipObjS(As, Bs)) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterNext(zipObjS(.IntSeq, _:IntSeq))              => #iterDone ... </k>\n  rule <k> #iterNext(zipObjS(iCons(_:Int, _:IntSeq), .IntSeq)) => #iterDone ... </k>\n\n  // ==== range(stop) / range(start, stop) / range(start, stop, step) =========\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"range\", I:Int, .Vals)               => rangeObj(0, I, 1)\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"range\", A:Int, B:Int, .Vals)        => rangeObj(A, B, 1)\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"range\", A:Int, B:Int, S:Int, .Vals) => rangeObj(A, B, S)\n       requires S =/=Int 0\n\n  // ==== eval(str) — arithmetic expressions (ints, + - * // **, spaces) ========\n  // Python precedence: ** right-assoc, then * //, then + -. Promoted from 160's\n  // trusted pass evaluator, now DEFINED in the reference and driven by a\n  // code-level tokenizer. Reduces on concrete strings (krun); a symbolic\n  // argument leaves the call unevaluated for problem-level folds.\n  rule applyBuiltin(\"eval\", str(CS:IntSeq), .Vals) => evalArith(CS)\n  syntax Int ::= evalArith(IntSeq) [function]\n  rule evalArith(CS:IntSeq)\n    => firstNdE(passAddE(passMulE(passPowE(tokOps(CS), tokNds(CS)))))\n\n  syntax OpSeq ::= \".OpSeq\" | oCons(String, OpSeq)\n\n  syntax Bool ::= evDigit(Int) [function, total]\n  rule evDigit(C:Int) => C >=Int 48 andBool C <=Int 57\n  syntax Bool ::= evHead42(IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule evHead42(iCons(42, _:IntSeq)) => true\n  rule evHead42(_:IntSeq)            => false [owise]\n  syntax Bool ::= evHead47(IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule evHead47(iCons(47, _:IntSeq)) => true\n  rule evHead47(_:IntSeq)            => false [owise]\n\n  syntax OpSeq ::= tokOps(IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule tokOps(.IntSeq)                 => .OpSeq\n  rule tokOps(iCons(32, R:IntSeq))     => tokOps(R)\n  rule tokOps(iCons(C:Int, R:IntSeq))  => tokOps(R) requires evDigit(C)\n  rule tokOps(iCons(42, iCons(42, R:IntSeq))) => oCons(\"**\", tokOps(R))\n  rule tokOps(iCons(42, R:IntSeq))     => oCons(\"*\", tokOps(R)) requires notBool evHead42(R)\n  rule tokOps(iCons(47, iCons(47, R:IntSeq))) => oCons(\"//\", tokOps(R))\n  rule tokOps(iCons(47, R:IntSeq))     => oCons(\"/\", tokOps(R)) requires notBool evHead47(R)\n  rule tokOps(iCons(43, R:IntSeq))     => oCons(\"+\", tokOps(R))\n  rule tokOps(iCons(45, R:IntSeq))     => oCons(\"-\", tokOps(R))\n\n  syntax IntSeq ::= tokNds(IntSeq) [function, total]\n                  | tokNdAcc(Int, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule tokNds(.IntSeq)                => .IntSeq\n  rule tokNds(iCons(32, R:IntSeq))    => tokNds(R)\n  rule tokNds(iCons(C:Int, R:IntSeq)) => tokNdAcc(C -Int 48, R) requires evDigit(C)\n  rule tokNds(iCons(C:Int, R:IntSeq)) => tokNds(R)\n       requires notBool evDigit(C) andBool C =/=Int 32\n  rule tokNdAcc(A:Int, iCons(C:Int, R:IntSeq)) => tokNdAcc(A *Int 10 +Int (C -Int 48), R)\n       requires evDigit(C)\n  rule tokNdAcc(A:Int, S:IntSeq) => iCons(A, tokNds(S)) [owise]\n\n  syntax EvPair ::= evp(OpSeq, IntSeq)\n  syntax Int ::= firstNdE(EvPair) [function, total]\n  rule firstNdE(evp(_:OpSeq, iCons(N:Int, _:IntSeq))) => N\n  rule firstNdE(_:EvPair) => 0 [owise]\n\n  syntax Int ::= applyOpE(String, Int, Int) [function, total]\n  rule applyOpE(\"+\",  A:Int, B:Int) => A +Int B\n  rule applyOpE(\"-\",  A:Int, B:Int) => A -Int B\n  rule applyOpE(\"*\",  A:Int, B:Int) => A *Int B\n  rule applyOpE(\"//\", A:Int, B:Int) => A divInt B\n  rule applyOpE(\"**\", A:Int, B:Int) => A ^Int B\n  rule applyOpE(_:String, A:Int, _:Int) => A [owise]\n\n  syntax EvPair ::= passPowE(OpSeq, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule passPowE(.OpSeq, NDS:IntSeq) => evp(.OpSeq, NDS)\n  rule passPowE(oCons(\"**\", OPS:OpSeq), iCons(N:Int, NDS:IntSeq)) => powCombE(N, passPowE(OPS, NDS))\n  rule passPowE(oCons(O:String, OPS:OpSeq), iCons(N:Int, NDS:IntSeq)) => powCarryE(O, N, passPowE(OPS, NDS))\n       requires O =/=String \"**\"\n  rule passPowE(_:OpSeq, .IntSeq) => evp(.OpSeq, .IntSeq) [owise]\n  syntax EvPair ::= powCombE(Int, EvPair) [function, total]\n  rule powCombE(N:Int, evp(OPS:OpSeq, iCons(M:Int, REST:IntSeq))) => evp(OPS, iCons(N ^Int M, REST))\n  rule powCombE(N:Int, evp(OPS:OpSeq, .IntSeq)) => evp(OPS, iCons(N, .IntSeq))\n  syntax EvPair ::= powCarryE(String, Int, EvPair) [function, total]\n  rule powCarryE(O:String, N:Int, evp(OPS:OpSeq, NDS:IntSeq)) => evp(oCons(O, OPS), iCons(N, NDS))\n\n  syntax EvPair ::= passMulE(EvPair) [function, total] | passAddE(EvPair) [function, total]\n  rule passMulE(evp(OPS:OpSeq, iCons(N0:Int, NDS:IntSeq))) => passLGoE(\"mul\", N0, OPS, NDS, .OpSeq, .IntSeq)\n  rule passMulE(evp(OPS:OpSeq, .IntSeq)) => evp(OPS, .IntSeq)\n  rule passAddE(evp(OPS:OpSeq, iCons(N0:Int, NDS:IntSeq))) => passLGoE(\"add\", N0, OPS, NDS, .OpSeq, .IntSeq)\n  rule passAddE(evp(OPS:OpSeq, .IntSeq)) => evp(OPS, .IntSeq)\n  syntax EvPair ::= passLGoE(String, Int, OpSeq, IntSeq, OpSeq, IntSeq) [function, total]\n  rule passLGoE(_:String, CUR:Int, .OpSeq, _:IntSeq, OO:OpSeq, ON:IntSeq) => evp(OO, appendIE(ON, CUR))\n  rule passLGoE(L:String, CUR:Int, oCons(O:String, OPS:OpSeq), iCons(N:Int, NDS:IntSeq), OO:OpSeq, ON:IntSeq)\n    => passLGoE(L, applyOpE(O, CUR, N), OPS, NDS, OO, ON)\n       requires inLevelE(L, O)\n  rule passLGoE(L:String, CUR:Int, oCons(O:String, OPS:OpSeq), iCons(N:Int, NDS:IntSeq), OO:OpSeq, ON:IntSeq)\n\n===== comp =====\n// reference/src/semantics/comprehension.k — MPY-COMPREHENSION\n\nmodule MPY-COMPREHENSION\n  imports MPY-CORE\n  imports MPY-OPERATORS\n  imports MPY-LIST\n  imports MPY-CONTROLS\n  imports MPY-FUNCTIONS\n\n  // A comprehension is pure syntactic sugar\n  rule ListComp(ELT:Expr, Gs:CompFors) => Call(closureExpr(.ParamNames, compBody(Gs, ELT)), .Exprs)\n  rule GenExp(ELT:Expr, Gs:CompFors)   => Call(closureExpr(.ParamNames, compBody(Gs, ELT)), .Exprs)\n\n  syntax Stmts ::= compBody(CompFors, Expr) [macro]\n  rule compBody(Gs:CompFors, ELT:Expr)\n    => Assign(Name(\"$acc\"), ListExpr(.Exprs)) compNest(Gs, ELT) Return(Name(\"$acc\"))\n\n  syntax Stmt ::= compNest(CompFors, Expr) [macro-rec]\n  rule compNest(.CompFors, ELT:Expr)\n    => Assign(Name(\"$acc\"), BinOp(\"+\", Name(\"$acc\"), ListExpr(ELT)))\n  rule compNest((CompFor(T:Expr, ITER:Expr, Fs:Exprs) GRest:CompFors), ELT:Expr)\n    => For(T, ITER, If(compGuard(Fs), compNest(GRest, ELT), .Stmts))\n\n  syntax Expr ::= compGuard(Exprs) [macro]\n  rule compGuard(.Exprs)             => Bool(true)\n  rule compGuard((F:Expr, Fs:Exprs)) => BoolOp(\"and\", (F, Fs))\nendmodule\n\n===== syntax =====\n// reference/src/semantics/syntax.k — MPY-SYNTAX\n\nmodule MPY-SYNTAX\n  imports INT-SYNTAX\n  imports FLOAT-SYNTAX\n  imports BOOL-SYNTAX\n  imports STRING-SYNTAX\n\n  syntax Expr ::= \"Int\"      \"(\" Int \")\"\n                | \"Float\"    \"(\" Float \")\"\n                | \"Bool\"     \"(\" Bool \")\"\n                | \"Name\"     \"(\" String \")\"\n                | \"Str\"      \"(\" String \")\"\n                | \"UnaryOp\"  \"(\" String \",\" Expr \")\" [strict(2)]\n                | \"BinOp\"    \"(\" String \",\" Expr \",\" Expr \")\" [seqstrict(2, 3)]\n                | \"BoolOp\"    \"(\" String \",\" Exprs \")\"\n                | \"ListExpr\"  \"(\" Exprs \")\"\n                | \"DictExpr\"  \"(\" Entries \")\"\n                | \"ListComp\"  \"(\" Expr \",\" CompFors \")\" [macro]\n                | \"GenExp\"    \"(\" Expr \",\" CompFors \")\" [macro]\n                | \"TupleExpr\" \"(\" Exprs \")\"\n                | \"Subscript\" \"(\" Expr \",\" Index \")\"\n                | \"IfExp\"     \"(\" Expr \",\" Expr \",\" Expr \")\" [strict(1)]\n                | \"Lambda\"    \"(\" Params \",\" Expr \")\"\n                | \"KwArg\"     \"(\" String \",\" Expr \")\"\n                | \"Lambda\"    \"(\" Params \",\" CellVars \",\" FreeVars \",\" Expr \")\"\n                | \"NoneVal\"\n                | \"Call\"      \"(\" Expr \",\" Exprs \")\"\n                | \"Attribute\" \"(\" Expr \",\" String \")\" [strict(1)]\n                | \"Compare\"   \"(\" Expr \",\" CmpOp \")\"\n\n  syntax CmpOp    ::= \"CmpOp\" \"(\" String \",\" Expr \")\"\n  syntax Entry    ::= \"Entry\" \"(\" Expr \",\" Expr \")\"\n  syntax Entries  ::= List{Entry, \",\"}\n  syntax CompFor  ::= \"CompFor\" \"(\" Expr \",\" Expr \",\" Exprs \")\"\n  syntax CompFors ::= List{CompFor, \"\"}\n  syntax Exprs    ::= List{Expr, \",\"}\n  syntax Index    ::= Expr | \"Slice\" \"(\" Bound \",\" Bound \",\" Bound \")\"\n  syntax Bound    ::= Expr | \"NoBound\"\n\n  syntax Stmt ::= \"Assign\"    \"(\" Expr \",\" Expr \")\" [strict(2)]\n                | \"Import\"    \"(\" String \")\"\n                | \"ImportFrom\" \"(\" String \",\" ParamNames \")\"\n                | \"AugAssign\" \"(\" Expr \",\" String \",\" Expr \")\" [strict(3)]\n                | \"For\"       \"(\" Expr \",\" Expr \",\" Stmts \")\" [strict(2)]\n                | \"While\"     \"(\" Expr \",\" Stmts \")\"\n                | \"Break\"\n                | \"Continue\"\n                | \"If\"        \"(\" Expr \",\" Stmts \",\" Stmts \")\" [strict(1)]\n                | \"Return\"    \"(\" Expr \")\" [strict]\n                | \"Assert\"    \"(\" Expr \")\" [strict]\n                | \"Expr\"      \"(\" Expr \")\" [strict]\n                | \"FuncDef\"   \"(\" String \",\" Params \",\" Stmts \")\"\n                | \"FuncDef\"   \"(\" String \",\" Params \",\" CellVars \",\" FreeVars \",\" Stmts \")\"\n\n  syntax Stmts      ::= List{Stmt, \"\"}\n  syntax Params     ::= \"Params\" \"(\" ParamNames \")\"\n  syntax CellVars   ::= \"CellVars\" \"(\" ParamNames \")\"\n  syntax FreeVars   ::= \"FreeVars\" \"(\" ParamNames \")\"\n  syntax ParamNames ::= List{String, \",\"}\n  syntax Module     ::= \"Module\" \"(\" Stmts \")\"\nendmodule\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
38:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:34:27.534Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_088a97266d078c35016a644adf72b08196bb2aa60f831da12c","status":"completed","call_id":"call_ojBrLrgZ1inHWAVRvLkwSoNH","name":"exec","input":"const qs = [\n[\"functions1\",\"sed -n '1,180p' reference-semantics/semantics/functions.k\"],\n[\"functions2\",\"sed -n '181,360p' reference-semantics/semantics/functions.k\"],\n[\"call\",\"sed -n '1,320p' reference-semantics/semantics/call.k\"],\n[\"bool\",\"sed -n '1,280p' reference-semantics/semantics/bool.k\"],\n[\"int\",\"sed -n '1,300p' reference-semantics/semantics/int.k\"],\n[\"versions\",\"command -v kup || true; command -v kompile; kompile --version; kprove --version; krun --version\"],\n[\"agents\",\"find /workspace -name AGENTS.md -print\"]\n];\nfor (const [name,cmd] of qs) {\n  const r=await tools.exec_command({cmd,workdir:\"/workspace\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:20000});\n  text(`===== ${name} =====\\n${r.output}`);\n}\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
39:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:34:28.047Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_ojBrLrgZ1inHWAVRvLkwSoNH","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== functions1 =====\n// reference/src/semantics/functions.k — MPY-FUNCTIONS\n\nmodule MPY-FUNCTIONS\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  // call routing + callee/arg evaluation (#callee/#args/#argCont) live in call.k;\n  // this module owns the frame lifecycle (bind params, return, pop).\n  syntax KItem ::= frame(continuation: K, callerEnv: Int, savedLoc: Int)\n                 | #bindP(ParamNames, Vals)\n                 | \"#pop\"\n                 | \"#endcall\"\n\n  // ==== def / anonymous closure =============================================\n  rule <k> FuncDef(F:String, Params(PNS:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts) => .K ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ F <- closureVal(PNS, BODY, L) ], _) ... </scopes>\n\n  syntax Expr ::= closureExpr(ParamNames, Stmts)\n  rule <k> closureExpr(PNS:ParamNames, BODY:Stmts) => closureVal(PNS, BODY, L) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n\n  // ==== annotated def/lambda (closure cells; spec 2.3) ======================\n  // closureValC(params, cellvars, body, captured-cells). No frame anchor: all\n  // enclosing-local reads are freevars (symtable-complete) and go through the\n  // captured cells; everything else is global/builtin, so the callee frame's\n  // parent is the module scope (0) — sound after the defining frame dies.\n  syntax Val ::= closureValC(ParamNames, ParamNames, Stmts, Map)\n\n  // capture: resolve each freevar to the enclosing frame's cellRef, then bind\n  // (FuncDef) or yield (Lambda) the closure value.\n  syntax KItem ::= #mkClosure(String, ParamNames, ParamNames, ParamNames, Stmts, Map)\n                 | #mkLambda(ParamNames, ParamNames, ParamNames, Stmts, Map)\n  rule <k> FuncDef(F:String, Params(PNS:ParamNames), CellVars(CVS:ParamNames),\n                   FreeVars(FVS:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts)\n        => #mkClosure(F, PNS, CVS, FVS, BODY, .Map) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #mkClosure(F:String, PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames,\n                      (FV:String, FVR:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)\n        => #mkClosure(F, PNS, CVS, FVR, BODY, CM [ FV <- {M[FV]}:>Val ]) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       requires FV in_keys(M)\n  rule <k> #mkClosure(F:String, PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames,\n                      .ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, CM:Map) => .K ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ F <- closureValC(PNS, CVS, BODY, CM) ], _) ... </scopes>\n\n  rule <k> Lambda(Params(PNS:ParamNames), E:Expr)\n        => closureVal(PNS, Return(E) .Stmts, L) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n  rule <k> Lambda(Params(PNS:ParamNames), CellVars(CVS:ParamNames),\n                  FreeVars(FVS:ParamNames), E:Expr)\n        => #mkLambda(PNS, CVS, FVS, Return(E) .Stmts, .Map) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #mkLambda(PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames,\n                     (FV:String, FVR:ParamNames), BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)\n        => #mkLambda(PNS, CVS, FVR, BODY, CM [ FV <- {M[FV]}:>Val ]) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       requires FV in_keys(M)\n  rule <k> #mkLambda(PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames, .ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)\n        => closureValC(PNS, CVS, BODY, CM) ... </k>\n\n  // ==== bind params ========================================================\n  rule <k> #bindP(.ParamNames, .Vals) => .K ... </k>\n  rule <k> #bindP((P:String, PS:ParamNames), (V:Val, VS:Vals)) => #bindP(PS, VS) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ P <- V ], _) ... </scopes>\n  // a param that is a cellvar was pre-bound to its cell at frame entry\n  rule <k> #bindP((P:String, PS:ParamNames), (V:Val, VS:Vals))\n        => #cellW({M[P]}:>Val, V) ~> #bindP(PS, VS) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map, _) ... </scopes>\n       requires \"$cells\" in_keys(M)\n        andBool pnMember(P, cellsOf({M[\"$cells\"]}:>Val))\n        andBool P in_keys(M) andBool isCellRef({M[P]}:>Val)\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  // ==== return / pop the frame (the returned expr evaluates by strictness) ==\n  rule <k> Return(V:Val) ~> _ => #pop </k>\n       <ret> noRet => retV(V) </ret>\n  rule <k> #endcall => #pop ... </k>\n       <ret> noRet => retV(noneV) </ret>\n  // pop: restore env, DEALLOCATE the callee frame, and wind scopeLoc back (LIFO allocation\n  // makes the saved loc the callee frame's own loc). Sound because no closureVal outlives its\n  // defining frame (frontend subset: no returned/stored closures; module defs live in loc 0).\n  rule <k> #pop => V ~> CONT </k>\n       <ret>   retV(V) => noRet </ret>\n       <stack> ListItem(frame(CONT:K, CALLERL:Int, SAVEDL:Int)) => .List ... </stack>\n       <env>   L:Int => CALLERL </env>\n       <scopes> SC:Map => SC [ L <- undef ] </scopes>\n       <scopeLoc> _ => SAVEDL </scopeLoc>\nendmodule\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== functions2 =====\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== call =====\n// reference/src/semantics/call.k — MPY-CALL\n// ONE call route for every callable: evaluate the callee to a VALUE (name lookup reaches\n// builtins at the root; a cooled Attribute is a bound method), evaluate the args (the shared\n// #evalArgs loop in core.k), then dispatch on the callee value:\n//   closureVal    -> bind params, push a frame (user function)\n//   boundMethodV  -> applyMethod\n//   builtinV      -> the #iterNext folds (sum/all/any/max/min) or applyBuiltin\n//   typeV         -> callable type objects: int(...) / str(...)\n\nmodule MPY-CALL\n  imports MPY-METHODS\n  imports MPY-BUILTINS\n  imports MPY-FUNCTIONS\n\n  // a cooled attribute is a bound method value\n  rule <k> Attribute(V:Val, M:String) => boundMethodV(V, M) ... </k>\n\n  // evaluate callee, then args ([owise]: problem-local Call interceptions beat this)\n  syntax KItem ::= #callee(Exprs)\n  rule <k> Call(Fe:Expr, ARGS:Exprs) => Fe ~> #callee(ARGS) ... </k> [owise]\n  rule <k> CV:Val ~> #callee(ARGS:Exprs) => #evalArgs(ARGS, .Vals, toCall(CV)) ... </k>\n\n  // ==== dispatch on the callee value ========================================\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(OBJ:Val, M:String)), ACC:Vals) => applyMethod(OBJ, M, ACC) ... </k>\n\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"sum\")), (OBJ:Iterable, .Vals)) => #sumAcc(OBJ, 0) ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"all\")), (OBJ:Iterable, .Vals)) => #allAcc(OBJ)    ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"any\")), (OBJ:Iterable, .Vals)) => #anyAcc(OBJ)    ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"max\")), (OBJ:Iterable, .Vals)) => #maxAcc0(OBJ)   ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(\"min\")), (OBJ:Iterable, .Vals)) => #minAcc0(OBJ)   ... </k>\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(BN:String)), ACC:Vals) => applyBuiltin(BN, ACC) ... </k> [owise]\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(typeV(T:String)),     ACC:Vals) => applyBuiltin(T, ACC)  ... </k>\n\n  // ==== heap-object arguments/receivers =====================================\n  // Builtins and type calls READ structure — deref the first two arg positions\n  // (covers len/sum/all/any/max/min/sorted/list/set + zip's pair). Mutating list\n  // methods take the ref itself; every other method receiver is deref'd.\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(BN:String)), (ref(H:Int), REST:Vals))\n        => #applyK(toCall(builtinV(BN)), (V, REST)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(builtinV(BN:String)), (A:Val, ref(H:Int), REST:Vals))\n        => #applyK(toCall(builtinV(BN)), (A, V, REST)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool isRefV(A)\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(typeV(T:String)), (ref(H:Int), REST:Vals))\n        => #applyK(toCall(typeV(T)), (V, REST)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  syntax Bool ::= isMutMethod(String) [function, total]\n  rule isMutMethod(M:String)\n    => M ==String \"append\" orBool M ==String \"sort\" orBool M ==String \"extend\"\n       orBool M ==String \"insert\" orBool M ==String \"pop\" orBool M ==String \"remove\"\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(ref(H:Int), M:String)), ACC:Vals)\n        => #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(V, M)), ACC) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool isMutMethod(M)\n       [priority(40)]\n  // non-mutating methods READ their heap-object arguments too (join's list);\n  // mutators keep refs (append of a list into a list-of-lists stays aliased)\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(OBJ:Val, M:String)), (ref(H:Int), REST:Vals))\n        => #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(OBJ, M)), (V, REST)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool isMutMethod(M) andBool notBool isRefV(OBJ)\n       [priority(40)]\n\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(closureVal(PNS:ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, DEFL:Int)), ACC:Vals) ~> CONT\n        => #bindP(PNS, ACC) ~> BODY ~> #endcall </k>\n       <env>     CALLERL:Int => NEWL </env>\n       <scopes>   STORE:Map => STORE [ NEWL <- scope(.Map, parent(DEFL)) ] </scopes>\n       <scopeLoc> NEWL:Int => NEWL +Int 1 </scopeLoc>\n       <stack>   .List => ListItem(frame(CONT, CALLERL, NEWL)) ... </stack>\n\n  // annotated closure: the frame starts with the captured freevar cells, its\n  // parent is the module scope (all enclosing-local reads go through cells),\n  // and the cellvars' fresh cells allocate before params bind (a cellvar param\n  // then writes through its cell in #bindP).\n  rule <k> #applyK(toCall(closureValC(PNS:ParamNames, CVS:ParamNames, BODY:Stmts, CM:Map)), ACC:Vals) ~> CONT\n        => #allocCells(CVS) ~> #bindP(PNS, ACC) ~> BODY ~> #endcall </k>\n       <env>     CALLERL:Int => NEWL </env>\n       <scopes>   STORE:Map => STORE [ NEWL <- scope(CM [ \"$cells\" <- cellsMark(CVS) ], parent(0)) ] </scopes>\n       <scopeLoc> NEWL:Int => NEWL +Int 1 </scopeLoc>\n       <stack>   .List => ListItem(frame(CONT, CALLERL, NEWL)) ... </stack>\n\n  syntax KItem ::= #allocCells(ParamNames)\n  rule <k> #allocCells(.ParamNames) => .K ... </k>\n  rule <k> #allocCells((CV:String, R:ParamNames)) => #allocCells(R) ... </k>\n       <env> L:Int </env>\n       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ CV <- cellRef(N) ], _) ... </scopes>\n       <heap>    H:Map => (N |-> cellV(noneV)) H </heap>\n       <heapLoc> N:Int => N +Int 1 </heapLoc>\n       requires notBool N in_keys(H)\nendmodule\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== bool =====\n// reference/src/semantics/bool.k — MPY-BOOL\n// Bool operator cases + the truthy boolean operations (not / and / or; and-or are\n// value-returning short-circuit over any Vals, per Python).\n\nmodule MPY-BOOL\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  rule applyUn(\"not\", V:Val) => notBool truthy(V)\n\n  rule applyCmp(\"==\", B1:Bool, B2:Bool) => B1 ==Bool B2\n  rule applyCmp(\"!=\", B1:Bool, B2:Bool) => B1 =/=Bool B2\n\n  // ==== BoolOp: short-circuit, value-returning and / or =====================\n  // the node is its own accumulator: heat the HEAD element only, then either return it\n  // (short-circuit) or drop it and continue\n  context BoolOp(_, (HOLE:Expr, _:Exprs))\n  rule <k> BoolOp(_:String, (V:Val, .Exprs)) => V ... </k>\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"and\", (V:Val, A:Expr, REST:Exprs)) => BoolOp(\"and\", (A, REST)) ... </k>\n       requires truthy(V)\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"and\", (V:Val, _:Expr, _:Exprs)) => V ... </k>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"or\",  (V:Val, _:Expr, _:Exprs)) => V ... </k>\n       requires truthy(V)\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"or\",  (V:Val, A:Expr, REST:Exprs)) => BoolOp(\"or\", (A, REST)) ... </k>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n\n  // ==== heap-object head: decide truthiness THROUGH the heap, never rewrite the\n  // operand — and/or return the OBJECT itself (Python identity), not its structure\n  rule <k> BoolOp(_:String, (ref(H:Int), .Exprs)) => ref(H) ... </k>\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"and\", (ref(H:Int), A:Expr, REST:Exprs)) => BoolOp(\"and\", (A, REST)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires truthy(V)\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"and\", (ref(H:Int), _:Expr, _:Exprs)) => ref(H) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"or\", (ref(H:Int), _:Expr, _:Exprs)) => ref(H) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires truthy(V)\n       [priority(40)]\n  rule <k> BoolOp(\"or\", (ref(H:Int), A:Expr, REST:Exprs)) => BoolOp(\"or\", (A, REST)) ... </k>\n       <heap> ... H |-> V:Val ... </heap>\n       requires notBool truthy(V)\n       [priority(40)]\nendmodule\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== int =====\n// reference/src/semantics/int.k — MPY-INT\n// Int cases of the operator dispatch; Python // and % are floored (pyMod).\n\nmodule MPY-INT\n  imports MPY-CORE\n\n  rule applyUn(\"-\", I:Int) => 0 -Int I\n\n  rule applyBin(\"+\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 +Int I2\n  // Bool participates in int arithmetic (x += (a == b))\n  rule applyBin(\"+\",  I:Int, B:Bool) => I +Int #if B #then 1 #else 0 #fi\n  rule applyBin(\"+\",  B:Bool, I:Int) => #if B #then 1 #else 0 #fi +Int I\n  rule applyBin(\"-\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 -Int I2\n  rule applyBin(\"*\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 *Int I2\n  rule applyBin(\"%\",  I1:Int, I2:Int) => pyMod(I1, I2)\n  rule applyBin(\"//\", I1:Int, I2:Int) => (I1 -Int pyMod(I1, I2)) /Int I2\n  rule applyBin(\"**\", I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 ^Int I2 requires I2 >=Int 0\n\n  syntax Int ::= pyMod(Int, Int) [function]\n  rule pyMod(I1:Int, I2:Int) => ((I1 %Int I2) +Int I2) %Int I2\n\n  rule applyCmp(\"<\",  I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 <Int  I2\n  rule applyCmp(\"<=\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 <=Int I2\n  rule applyCmp(\">\",  I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 >Int  I2\n  rule applyCmp(\">=\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 >=Int I2\n  rule applyCmp(\"==\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 ==Int I2\n  rule applyCmp(\"!=\", I1:Int, I2:Int)   => I1 =/=Int I2\nendmodule\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== versions =====\n/usr/bin/kompile\nK version:    v7.1.293\nBuild date:   Fri Oct 03 13:32:35 CDT 2025\nK version:    v7.1.293\nBuild date:   Fri Oct 03 13:32:35 CDT 2025\nK version:    v7.1.293\nBuild date:   Fri Oct 03 13:32:35 CDT 2025\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"===== agents =====\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
47:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:35:09.653Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_088a97266d078c35016a644b0d14c48196b26c9e72a3824983","status":"completed","call_id":"call_lP9FWMFy3qTMWE5R7FAM2JUi","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"sed -n '1,460p' /codex-home/skills/k-proof-technique/SKILL.md\",\"workdir\":\"/workspace\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":40000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
48:{"timestamp":"2026-07-25T05:35:09.722Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_lP9FWMFy3qTMWE5R7FAM2JUi","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"---\nname: k-proof-technique\ndescription: 'Use when constructing a K reachability proof requires deriving a loop invariant, choosing a summary representation, laying out proof obligations, or abstracting an operation kprove cannot evaluate.'\n---\n\n## Scope & boundary\n\nThis skill is the **strategy** for a K reachability proof: how to derive a loop\ninvariant, why a summary must be solver-friendly, and what obligations the\nproof has to discharge.\n\nThis skill does **not** run any tool. Machine-checking the proof — running `kprove`, reading a residual configuration, adding a `[simplification]` lemma, narrowing with `--depth N` — belongs to `proving-spec`.\n\nThis skill does **not** execute or document a proof run. It supplies the\nreasoning scaffold that execution workflows consume.\n\n---\n\n## The scaffold\n\n### Deriving the loop invariant\n\nThe loop invariant captures what is preserved at the loop head on every iteration. To derive it:\n\n1. **Identify the postcondition.** For the entry claim it is the condition on the final state — e.g. `n == 0` and `s == sumTo(N0)`.\n\n2. **Ask: what is true at the loop head that implies the postcondition when the loop exits?** The loop exits when the guard is false (`n == 0`). At that point the invariant must reduce to the postcondition.\n\n3. **Parametrize by the mid-loop state.** Replace the initial values by symbolic variables `N` (remaining counter) and `S` (accumulated so far). The invariant is: \"with `n |-> N` and `s |-> S` at the loop head, the loop ends with `n |-> 0` and `s |-> S + sumTo(N)`.\"\n\n4. **Check the base case algebraically.** When `N == 0` the guard is false, the loop exits immediately, and the postcondition becomes `S == S + sumTo(0)`, i.e. `sumTo(0) == 0` — true by the definition of the summary function.\n\n5. **Check the inductive case algebraically.** When `N > 0`, one iteration runs: `n` becomes `N - 1`, `s` becomes `S + N`. Applying the invariant to the new state gives `n |-> 0` and `s |-> (S + N) + sumTo(N - 1)`. The obligation is `(S + N) + sumTo(N - 1) == S + sumTo(N)`, which the chosen closed form reduces to solver-supported integer arithmetic in this example.\n\nThe invariant ties the running partial sum `S` and the remaining count `N` to the final postcondition through the summary function `sumTo`. The summary function belongs in `verification.k`, not in the spec module.\n\n### Choose the summary representation\n\nWhen an equivalent closed form exists in a theory the target solver can decide,\nprefer it; for the sum example, `N*(N+1)/2` reduces the obligation to supported\ninteger arithmetic. If no faithful closed form exists, keep the recursive\ndefinition—it may be the only exact specification of the result. Make its base\nand step equations explicit, and use induction, folding, or summary lemmas so\nthe proof does not depend on unbounded evaluator unfolding. A recursive\nequation can otherwise keep expanding while its symbolic guard remains\nentailed; see\n[symbolic-recursion debugging](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/symbolic-recursion.md).\n\n### The three obligation shapes\n\nEvery loop-bearing K proof discharges three obligations:\n\n1. **Base case** — the invariant holds when the loop guard is already false: the postcondition it claims must follow from doing nothing.\n2. **Inductive case** — assuming the invariant at the loop head, one iteration of the loop body must re-establish it (or reach the postcondition, if the guard is now false).\n3. **Whole-program discharge** — instantiating the loop-invariant claim at the point the entry claim first reaches the loop head must produce exactly the entry claim's postcondition.\n\nA complete proof records how each obligation is discharged.\n\n---\n\n## Classify proof extensions before using them\n\nRead the [proof-extension soundness contract](../shared/proof-extension-soundness.md)\nbefore adding a proof-local function, equation, claim, or operational rewrite.\nClassify it as a definitional summary, derived lemma, operational bridge, or\ntrusted primitive.\n\nFor each extension, complete the contract's **Proof-extension record**. In\nparticular, answer whether it replaces a term that fixed semantics would have\nexecuted and enumerate the complete state footprint of that execution.\n\nProgram-defined code is not an external primitive. Either let fixed semantics\nexecute it or prove an auxiliary reachability claim connecting the exact body,\nbinding, arguments, environment, and state transition to the summary. A result\nequation alone does not justify skipping lookup, control, or effects.\n\nBefore admitting any operational bridge, prove a bridge-free universal\nconnection theorem over its complete match domain. The theorem must not import\nthe proposed bridge; it must use fixed semantics and independently justified\ntheory to establish the exact binding, execution, value, control, and state\ntransition for every configuration the bridge accepts. Finite tests do not\nsatisfy this precondition.\n\nOpacity is safe only when the proof is parametric in the primitive's value:\nchanging its interpretation must not establish a different source-level fact,\nexcept through an explicit external contract. A fresh symbol that selects a\nbranch, result, or observable state is a result-bearing oracle, not a threaded\nvalue. Reusing that symbol in both an operational bridge and postcondition is\ncircular unless a machine-checked theorem independently connects fixed\nexecution to its value.\n\nCheck every group of equations for guard coverage and overlap before using\n`[total]` or `[simplification]`. Guards that overlap must produce equal results\non the overlap. Narrow false off-domain equations instead of relying on current\nunreachability.\n\nIf Gate A rejects an extension, remove or disable it and inspect the\nfixed-semantics residual in the same agent invocation. Prefer fixed-semantics\nexecution, then an exact auxiliary execution theorem, then a stronger invariant\nor truthful definitional summary. Narrow the theorem honestly when that is the\nsound result; terminal incomplete reporting is reserved for an evidenced hard\nblocker, not for a proof strategy that is merely harder or slower. Repair that\nrequires redesign is not a hard blocker.\n\n---\n\n## Trusted-opaque primitives — isolating an external trust boundary\n\nUse opacity only for a fixed operation intentionally outside the theorem and\noutside the program-defined code being verified. Prove the surrounding structure\nand state the value-level result conditionally on the primitive's contract.\n\nOne K-specific implementation uses a `[function, total]` wrapper with **no rule\nthat fires under `kprove`** plus a `[concrete]` rule that computes the real value\nunder `krun`:\n\n```k\nsyntax Float ::= intFloatDiv(Int, Float) [function, total, no-evaluators]\nrule intFloatDiv(I, F) => Int2Float(I, 53, 11) /Float F  [concrete]\n```\n\n- Under `kprove` the argument is symbolic, the `[concrete]` rule does not fire, and\n  `intFloatDiv(I, F)` stays uninterpreted. The proof only *threads* that term — it never\n  reasons about the float value — so what is verified is the surrounding shape (the map, the\n  fold, the accumulator), position for position.\n- Under `krun` the argument is ground, the `[concrete]` rule fires, and the smoke/differential\n  test checks a real numeric answer (the LLVM backend has the float hooks).\n\n**`[no-evaluators]` is metadata, not a switch.** It suppresses the LLVM \"non-exhaustive match\"\nwarning on an under-covered total function and documents intent; it disables nothing. Opacity\nis an emergent property of the *rules*: no `kprove`-firing rule ⇒ opaque everywhere; a\n`[simplification]` rule with a guard ⇒ unfolds only where the guard is entailed; a `[concrete]`\nrule ⇒ krun computes it, kprove applies it only on ground arguments.\n\nOne consequence that bites:\n\n- A `[concrete]` rule **still fires on ground arguments under `kprove`** and hits the missing\n  hook — `subF(0.0, 1.0)` crashes just like `0.0 -Float 1.0`. Keep every argument symbolic and\n  emit any needed constant as a **literal** (`-1.0`), never as a computed expression.\n\nAn opaque term may be threaded without proving its value. Differential testing\ncan support the primitive's concrete implementation, but it does not replace an\nauxiliary theorem for program-defined code and does not establish a universal\nvalue property.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n- [Proof-extension soundness contract](../shared/proof-extension-soundness.md) —\n  classification, justification, and validation obligations for proof-local\n  functions, equations, claims, and rewrites.\n- [K functions, claims, and proof modules](../shared/k-claims.md) —\n  `[function]`, `[simplification]`, and claim syntax.\n- [Symbolic recursion](../shared/kprove-debug-troubleshoot/symbolic-recursion.md)\n  — diagnosis when a helper keeps unfolding.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f97c3-ce8b-7ec1-9e98-ff77780fcb0e"}}}
